OAI_PAPER({"slug": "The-equivariant-cohomological-Hikita-conjecture-for-arbitrary-quivers-September-24-2026", "title": "The equivariant cohomological Hikita conjecture for arbitrary quivers", "pdf": "https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/The-equivariant-cohomological-Hikita-conjecture-for-arbitrary-quivers-September-24-2026/main.pdf", "family": "044", "family_title": "The equivariant cohomological Hikita conjecture", "abstract_html": "We prove the equivariant cohomological Hikita conjecture for arbitrary finite quivers, including loops and multiple arrows. For any dimension and framing vectors, any commuting flavor torus, and a stability character whose semistable locus is stable and has free gauge action, the equivariant cohomology of the Nakajima variety is canonically isomorphic to the coordinate ring of the scheme-theoretic fixed locus of the stability cocharacter on the flavor-deformed Coulomb branch. This is an isomorphism of graded algebras over the common coefficient ring, retaining nilpotents and including the empty case.", "blocks": [{"label": "Theorem 1", "orig": "Theorem 1.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Both coefficient maps are surjective and have the same kernel.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">All cohomology and homology have complex coefficients. Bracketed references retain the numbering of the paper’s bibliography.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(Q=(Q_0,Q_1)\\) be a finite oriented quiver, allowing loops and multiple arrows. Fix complex vector spaces \\(V_i,W_i\\), of dimensions \\(v_i,w_i\\), and set\n  \\[\n  G=\\prod_{i\\in Q_0}\\mathrm{GL}(V_i),\\qquad\n  N=\\bigoplus_{a:i\\to j}\\mathrm{Hom}(V_i,V_j)\n       \\oplus\\bigoplus_{i\\in Q_0}\\mathrm{Hom}(V_i,W_i).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let an algebraic torus \\(F\\) act linearly on \\(N\\), commuting with \\(G\\), and write \\(\\mathfrak f=\\operatorname{Lie}F\\).</p><p class=\"li\">On \\(T^*N=N\\oplus N^*\\), use the cotangent moment map\n  \\[\n  \\langle\\mu(s,p),\\xi\\rangle=\\langle p,\\xi s\\rangle.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">A character \\(\\nu\\in X^*(G)\\) is called regular if the \\(\\nu\\)-semistable locus in \\(\\mu^{-1}(0)\\) equals the stable locus and \\(G\\) acts freely there. Fix such a character and put\n  \\[\n  D=\\mu^{-1}(0)^{\\nu\\text{-stable}},\\qquad X=D/G.\n  \\]\n  Empty \\(D\\) and \\(X\\) are allowed.</p><p class=\"li\">Put \\(\\mathcal O=\\mathbb C[[t]]\\), \\(\\mathcal K=\\mathbb C((t))\\), and define the BFN space and commutative flavor-deformed Coulomb algebra by\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal R=\n  \\{[g,s]\\in G(\\mathcal K)\\times^{G(\\mathcal O)}N(\\mathcal O):\n      gs\\in N(\\mathcal O)\\},\n  \\qquad\n  A_F=H_*^{G(\\mathcal O)\\times F}(\\mathcal R),\n  \\qquad\n  Y_{\\mathfrak f}=\\operatorname{Spec}A_F.\n  \\]\n  Here \\((g,s)\\sim(gh^{-1},hs)\\), multiplication is BFN convolution, and no loop-rotation deformation parameter is included.</p><p class=\"li\">The topological grading is by\n  \\[\n  \\pi_1(G)=\\mathbb Z^{\\{i:v_i&gt;0\\}},\n  \\qquad\n  T_{\\mathrm{top}}=\\operatorname{Hom}(\\pi_1(G),\\mathbb C^\\times).\n  \\]\n  Write \\(A_F^{(d)}\\) for topological charge \\(d\\). The character \\(\\nu\\) determines a cocharacter of \\(T_{\\mathrm{top}}\\), and charge \\(d\\) has cocharacter weight \\(\\nu\\cdot d\\).</p><p class=\"li\">The scheme-theoretic cocharacter-fixed quotient is\n  \\[\n  \\mathbb C[Y_{\\mathfrak f}^{\\nu}]\n  =A_F/\\langle(A_F)_m:m\\ne0\\rangle,\n  \\]\n  where \\((A_F)_m\\) denotes cocharacter weight \\(m\\). No reduction is taken.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(T\\subset G\\) be the diagonal maximal torus, with Weyl group \\(W\\), and put\n  \\[\n  R=H_{G\\times F}^*(\\mathrm{pt})\n    =\\mathbb C[\\operatorname{Lie}T\\oplus\\mathfrak f]^W,\n  \\qquad\n  R_0=H_G^*(\\mathrm{pt}).\n  \\]\n  The coefficient maps are\n  \\[\n  \\phi_H:R\\longrightarrow H_F^*(X),\\qquad\n  \\phi_C:R\\longrightarrow\\mathbb C[Y_{\\mathfrak f}^{\\nu}].\n  \\]\n  The first sends gauge Chern classes to tautological Chern classes and flavor parameters to their equivariant classes. The second is the Coulomb coefficient map followed by the fixed-scheme quotient.</p><p class=\"li\">A linear gauge or flavor parameter has degree two. The gradings being compared are ordinary cohomological degree and BFN homological degree.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Both coefficient maps are surjective and have the same kernel. They induce a canonical isomorphism of graded \\(R\\)-algebras\n\\[\nH_F^*(X)\\cong\\mathbb C[Y_{\\mathfrak f}^{\\nu}].\n\\]\nThe assertion retains nilpotents and includes the empty case, whose ring is zero. No finite-type, Dynkin, conical, or good/ugly hypothesis is imposed.</p>", "proof": "<p>Proposition 9 and Proposition 7 give graded \\(R\\)-algebra isomorphisms\n\\[\n\\mathbb C[Y_{\\mathfrak f}^{\\nu}]\n\\xrightarrow[\\sim]{\\beta}\nB_F\n\\xrightarrow[\\sim]{\\alpha}\nH_F^*(X),\n\\qquad\n(\\alpha\\circ\\beta)\\circ\\phi_C=\\phi_H.\n\\]\nTherefore\n\\[\n\\ker\\phi_C=\\ker\\phi_H.\n\\]\nProposition 1 gives surjectivity of the coefficient maps to \\(B_F\\) and \\(H_F^*(X)\\), and Proposition 9 identifies the former with the cocharacter-fixed quotient. Thus \\(\\phi_C\\) is also surjective.</p><p>The inverse of \\(\\alpha\\circ\\beta\\) is the asserted isomorphism. Surjectivity of \\(\\phi_H\\) makes it the unique isomorphism compatible with the coefficient maps. All quotients are scheme-theoretic, preserve the stated gradings, and retain nilpotents, including the zero ring in the empty case.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 1", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "After adding or removing gauge-trivial summands, one may suppose that \\(F\\) assigns a character to every arrow summand and acts diagonally on every framing multiplicity space.", "statement": "<p>Coefficient presentations, minuscule generation, and changes of auxiliary data.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the quiver, representation, flavor action, and regular stability of Theorem 1.</p><p class=\"li\">Vertices with \\(v_i=0\\) may be discarded. If all \\(v_i=0\\), both sides of Theorem 1 are \\(H_F^*(\\mathrm{pt})\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Write\n  \\[\n  P_F=\\mathbb C[x_{ia}:i\\in Q_0,\\ 1\\le a\\le v_i]\\otimes\\mathbb C[\\mathfrak f],\n  \\qquad\n  W=\\prod_i S_{v_i},\n  \\qquad R=P_F^W.\n  \\]\n  Each \\(x_{ia}\\) has degree two.</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(\\lambda=(\\lambda_{ia})\\in X_*(T)\\), put\n  \\[\n  [\\lambda]=\\left(\\sum_a\\lambda_{ia}\\right)_i,\n  \\qquad\n  W_\\lambda=\\operatorname{Stab}_W(\\lambda).\n  \\]\n  A positive minuscule coweight has all entries in \\(\\{0,1\\}\\); a negative minuscule coweight has all entries in \\(\\{0,-1\\}\\). Its orbit \\(\\operatorname{Gr}_G^\\lambda\\) is a closed, smooth product of Grassmannians.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\operatorname{wt}(N)\\) be the multiset of gauge-plus-flavor weights of \\(N\\), and put \\(u_+=\\max(u,0)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Define the full-topological-torus quotient\n  \\[\n  B_F=A_F/\\langle A_F^{(d)}:d\\ne0\\rangle.\n  \\]\n  Subscript \\(0\\) denotes trivial flavor.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p class=\"li num\">1. After adding or removing gauge-trivial summands, one may suppose that \\(F\\) assigns a character to every arrow summand and acts diagonally on every framing multiplicity space. These changes preserve stability and both fixed coefficient-algebra quotients.</p><p class=\"li num\">2. There is an injective, coefficient-preserving algebra map\n   \\[\n   A_F\\hookrightarrow\n   \\bigl(\\operatorname{Frac}(P_F)[X_*(T)]\\bigr)^W,\n   \\qquad e^\\lambda e^\\gamma=e^{\\lambda+\\gamma}.\n   \\]\n   Besides \\(R\\), the algebra is generated by all nonzero dressed positive and negative minuscule classes. For \\(p\\in P_F^{W_\\lambda}\\), their expressions are\n   \\[\n   M_{\\lambda,p}\n   =\n   \\sum_{\\lambda'\\in W\\lambda}\n   e^{\\lambda'}p_{\\lambda'}\n   \\frac{\\displaystyle\\prod_{\\beta\\in\\operatorname{wt}(N)}\n                 \\beta^{(-\\langle\\beta,\\lambda'\\rangle)_+}}\n        {\\operatorname{eu}_T(T_{t^{\\lambda'}}\\operatorname{Gr}_G^\\lambda)},\n   \\qquad p_{w\\lambda}=w(p).\n   \\tag{2.2}\n   \\]\n   Distinct translates occur once; pairings use only gauge components. These classes have charge \\([\\lambda]\\). On charge zero, BFN degree is twice ordinary polynomial degree of the rational coefficient.</p><p class=\"li num\">3. Both maps\n   \\[\n   R\\twoheadrightarrow B_F,\\qquad R\\twoheadrightarrow H_F^*(X)\n   \\]\n   are surjective. The quotient \\(B_F\\) is nonnegatively graded. Every homogeneous element of \\(\\ker(R\\to B_F)\\) is a finite sum of positive-length, topologically neutral words in nontrivial dressed minuscule generators, with coefficients in \\(R\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">4. The ordinary cohomology \\(H^*(X)\\) is finite-dimensional and even, and \\(H_F^*(X)\\) is free over \\(H_F^*(\\mathrm{pt})\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">5. If \\(F\\to F'\\) restricts a commuting \\(F'\\)-action on \\(N\\), then\n   \\[\n   A_{F'}\\otimes_{H_{F'}^*(\\mathrm{pt})}H_F^*(\\mathrm{pt})\n   \\cong A_F,\n   \\qquad\n   B_{F'}\\otimes_{H_{F'}^*(\\mathrm{pt})}H_F^*(\\mathrm{pt})\n   \\cong B_F.\n   \\]\n   These identifications preserve coefficients, both gradings, and convolution.</p><p class=\"li num\">6. A rational regular stability parameter has a neighborhood in real character space on which all rational parameters give the same stable and semistable loci. It can be perturbed, without changing the quotient or principal gauge bundle, to avoid every proper hyperplane\n   \\[\n   \\theta\\cdot b=0,\\qquad 0&lt;b\\le v.\n   \\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemmas 1.1–1.5 give the reductions, abelianization, flavor base change, local constancy, and Kirwan assertions.</p><p>Every nontrivial positive or negative minuscule generator has nonzero topological charge. The ideal\n\\[\n\\langle A_F^{(d)}:d\\ne0\\rangle\n\\]\nis generated by those classes: after killing them, the algebra is generated by \\(R\\) and supported in charge zero. Hence \\(R\\to B_F\\) is surjective and \\(B_F\\) is nonnegatively graded.</p><p>Expand a coefficient relation in this ideal as a finite sum of words in the minuscule generators and retain its topological-charge-zero component. Absorb coefficient factors into dressings. Degree by degree, this gives a finite sum of positive-length neutral words. Commutativity permits any ordering of their factors.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.1", "orig": "Lemma 2.1", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For the purposes of Theorem 1, one may assume that the flavor action assigns a character to each arrow summand and acts diagonally on each framing multiplicity space.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the setting of Proposition 1.</p><p class=\"li\">A representation \\(E\\) is gauge-trivial if \\(G\\) acts trivially on \\(E\\); its flavor action may be arbitrary.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For the purposes of Theorem 1, one may assume that the flavor action assigns a character to each arrow summand and acts diagonally on each framing multiplicity space. Adding or removing a gauge-trivial representation preserves both coefficient-algebra quotients and all stability conditions.</p>", "proof": "<p>Decompose \\(N\\) into \\(G\\)-isotypic summands. A commuting torus acts on each multiplicity space and is diagonalizable there. The nontrivial irreducible gauge representations occurring are the bifundamentals between distinct vertices, the dual standard representations from framing, and the traceless endomorphisms at vertices of rank at least two. All remaining summands are gauge-trivial. A weighted traceless endomorphism summand can be replaced by the full endomorphism representation by adding a scalar line of the same flavor weight.</p><p>If \\(E\\) is gauge-trivial, the moment map for \\(N\\oplus E\\) is the moment map for \\(N\\). Semi-invariant generation shows that the stable and semistable loci are the corresponding loci for \\(N\\), times \\(T^*E\\), with unchanged stabilizers. The quotient gains a factor \\(T^*E\\), which retracts \\(F\\)-equivariantly to zero.</p><p>In the space of triples, the \\(E(\\mathcal O)\\)-coordinate imposes no lattice constraint. It therefore gives a common vector-space factor in every finite truncation. The normalized equivariant Thom isomorphism removes this factor compatibly with convolution, both gradings, and the coefficient map.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.2", "orig": "Proposition 2.2", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The abelianization embedding, the minuscule formula (2.2), and generation by \\(R\\) and all nonzero dressed positive and negative minuscule classes hold as stated in Proposition 1.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use \\(P_F,W,W_\\lambda,\\operatorname{wt}(N)\\), and the minuscule coweights of Proposition 1.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The abelianization embedding, the minuscule formula (2.2), and generation by \\(R\\) and all nonzero dressed positive and negative minuscule classes hold as stated in Proposition 1. For homogeneous \\(p\\), if\n\\[\nc_\\lambda=\\sum_{\\beta\\in\\operatorname{wt}(N)}\n                  (-\\langle\\beta,\\lambda\\rangle)_+,\n\\qquad\na_\\lambda=\\dim\\operatorname{Gr}_G^\\lambda,\n\\]\nthen\n\\[\n\\deg M_{\\lambda,p}=2c_\\lambda-2a_\\lambda+\\deg p.\n\\]\nIn particular, on topological charge zero, BFN degree equals twice the ordinary polynomial degree of the rational coefficient in abelianization.</p>", "proof": "<p>Use classical BFN localization and the flavor construction [4, Proposition 3.22, Lemmas 5.9–5.13, Proposition 5.15, and Corollary 5.21], the minuscule formula [4, Proposition 6.6; 5, Appendix A, Proposition A.2], and quiver minuscule generation [42, Proposition 3.1 and Remarks 3.3–3.4].</p><p>The inclusion of the space of triples into the bundle of lattices \\(gN(\\mathcal O)\\) contributes the Euler class of\n\\[\nt^{\\lambda'}N(\\mathcal O)/\n\\bigl(N(\\mathcal O)\\cap t^{\\lambda'}N(\\mathcal O)\\bigr),\n\\]\nwhich is the numerator in (2.2). Localization on the closed minuscule orbit gives the tangent denominator. There is no rotation, so translation by \\(t^\\eta\\) changes the Laurent monomial without shifting coefficients. Localization and the orbit filtration give injectivity before taking Weyl invariants; the nonabelian image lies in the invariant part.</p><p>The minuscule coweights in the generation theorem are subsets of slots at several vertices at once. Successive level sets of positive or negative integral coweights give exactly the two specified families; single-slot generation is not asserted.</p><p>Lemma 1.1 puts arbitrary commuting flavors in weighted quiver form. Flavor characters change equivariant Euler factors, not the gauge-weight hyperplane arrangement in the generation proof. The same chamber argument retains those parameters, as discussed in [42, Section 1.6.1]. The embedding is classical localization, not specialization of a representation with loop rotation inverted.</p><p>The BFN homological normalization [4, Lemmas 2.2 and 2.5–2.6] gives\n\\[\n\\deg M_{\\lambda,p}=2c_\\lambda-2a_\\lambda+\\deg p.\n\\]\nThese are precisely the degrees of the numerator, tangent denominator, and dressing in (2.2). The bare matter-free torus class \\(e^\\lambda\\) has degree zero. General dressings are decomposed into homogeneous summands. No nonnegative grading on \\(A_F\\) itself is assumed.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.3", "orig": "Lemma 2.3", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The natural maps \\( A_{F'}\\otimes_{H_{F'}^*(\\mathrm{pt})}H_F^*(\\mathrm{pt})\\cong A_F, \\qquad B_{F'}\\otimes_{H_{F'}^*(\\mathrm{pt})}H_F^*(\\mathrm{pt})\\cong B_F \\) are isomorphisms respecting coefficient maps, both …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(F\\to F'\\) be a homomorphism of algebraic tori.</p><p class=\"li\">Assume the commuting \\(F\\)-action on \\(N\\) is the restriction of a commuting \\(F'\\)-action.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The natural maps\n\\[\nA_{F'}\\otimes_{H_{F'}^*(\\mathrm{pt})}H_F^*(\\mathrm{pt})\\cong A_F,\n\\qquad\nB_{F'}\\otimes_{H_{F'}^*(\\mathrm{pt})}H_F^*(\\mathrm{pt})\\cong B_F\n\\]\nare isomorphisms respecting coefficient maps, both gradings, and convolution.</p>", "proof": "<p>Filter the affine Grassmannian by bounded unions of orbits and truncate all section lattices between sufficiently high positive and negative powers of \\(t\\). Over an orbit, the truncated space of triples is a vector bundle of constant relative rank. The orbit is an affine-space bundle over a partial flag variety; its equivariant homology, with the relative shifts defining \\(A_F\\), is even. Its \\(F'\\)-equivariant homology is free over \\(H_{F'}^*(\\mathrm{pt})\\), and restricting flavor gives the asserted coefficient change on this stratum.</p><p>The exact sequences adjoining strata have no odd terms. The free successive quotients show that tensoring introduces no Tor term. Passing to stabilized truncations and their exhaustive union proves the assertion for \\(A_F\\). Pullback, refined intersection, and proper pushforward defining convolution commute with the change of equivariance.</p><p>Flavor parameters have charge zero. The ideal generated by all nonzero charge spaces after base change is the extension of the corresponding ideal before base change. Taking the quotient proves the assertion for \\(B_F\\); no flatness of \\(B_{F'}\\) is required.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.4", "orig": "Lemma 2.4", "depth": 1, "snippet": "There is a neighborhood \\(U\\) of \\(\\theta\\) in \\(X^*(G)\\otimes_\\mathbb Z\\mathbb R\\) such that the stable and semistable loci are unchanged for every rational \\(\\theta'\\in U\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\theta\\in X^*(G)\\otimes_\\mathbb Z\\mathbb Q\\) be a rational parameter for which every semistable point of \\(\\mu^{-1}(0)\\) is stable and has trivial stabilizer.</p><p class=\"li\">Positive integral multiples of rational parameters define the same stability condition.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a neighborhood \\(U\\) of \\(\\theta\\) in \\(X^*(G)\\otimes_\\mathbb Z\\mathbb R\\) such that the stable and semistable loci are unchanged for every rational \\(\\theta'\\in U\\). One may choose \\(\\theta'\\) outside all proper hyperplanes\n\\[\n\\theta'\\cdot b=0,\\qquad 0&lt;b\\le v,\n\\]\nand then clear denominators by a positive integer. Empty loci are included.</p>", "proof": "<p>Fix a maximal torus of \\(G\\) and its finite set of weights on \\(N\\oplus N^*\\). For a point \\(x\\) and \\(g\\in G\\), let \\(C(gx)\\) be the cone generated by the weights occurring in \\(gx\\). The affine Hilbert–Mumford criterion says that \\(x\\) is semistable precisely when the character belongs to every such cone, and stable with finite stabilizer precisely when it belongs to all their full interiors in real weight space [27; 22, Propositions 2.5 and 2.7].</p><p>Only finitely many cones occur. A sufficiently small neighborhood of \\(\\theta\\) remains inside each cone whose interior contains \\(\\theta\\), and outside each cone not containing \\(\\theta\\). Thus stable points remain stable and unstable points remain unstable. Regularity accounts for every point of \\(\\mu^{-1}(0)\\), including when all points are unstable.</p><p>The geometric quotient, principal gauge bundle, and freeness are unchanged. Choose a rational point avoiding the finitely many specified proper hyperplanes and rescale positively to clear denominators. If \\(G\\) is trivial, character space is a point and the list of bounded nonzero dimension vectors is empty.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.5", "orig": "the Kirwan-surjectivity argument following Lemma 2.4", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The Kirwan map \\( R\\twoheadrightarrow H_F^*(X) \\) is surjective.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the setting of Proposition 1, including regular stability.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The Kirwan map\n\\[\nR\\twoheadrightarrow H_F^*(X)\n\\]\nis surjective. Ordinary cohomology is finite-dimensional and even, and \\(H_F^*(X)\\) is free over \\(H_F^*(\\mathrm{pt})\\), with a basis represented by equivariant lifts of polynomials in tautological Chern classes.</p>", "proof": "<p>For nonempty \\(X\\), Lemma 1.4 gives a rational perturbation \\(\\theta\\) preserving the entire stable locus and avoiding \\(\\theta\\cdot b=0\\) for \\(0&lt;b\\le v\\). The extension\n\\[\n(\\theta,-\\theta\\cdot v)\n\\]\nis nondegenerate for the framed dimension vector \\((v,1)\\): a proper subvector has final coordinate zero, or its nonzero complement does. Clearing denominators preserves stability.</p><p>The quotient, principal gauge bundle, and tautological bundles are unchanged. Ordinary Kirwan surjectivity applies with the same coefficient map [32, Definition 3.1 and Theorem 1.2; 13, Section 2.1].</p><p>For arbitrary commuting \\(F\\), choose a homogeneous basis of \\(H^*(X)\\) represented by polynomials in ordinary tautological Chern classes. These lift to equivariant Chern classes. Leray–Hirsch for\n\\[\nX\\longrightarrow EF\\times_F X\\longrightarrow BF\n\\]\nmakes \\(H_F^*(X)\\) free over \\(H_F^*(\\mathrm{pt})\\) on these lifts and proves equivariant surjectivity, as in [32, Corollary 1.5]. In particular, \\(H^*(X)\\) is finite-dimensional and even. For empty \\(X\\), the assertion is the surjection to the zero ring.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 2", "orig": "Proposition 4.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The coefficient map induces a graded \\(R\\)-algebra surjection \\( B_F\\twoheadrightarrow H_F^*(X). \\) Equivalently, \\( \\ker(R\\to B_F)\\subseteq\\ker(R\\to H_F^*(X)). \\)", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the data and regular stability of Theorem 1.</p><p class=\"li\">Let\n  \\[\n  B_F=A_F/\\langle A_F^{(d)}:d\\ne0\\rangle.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The coefficient map induces a graded \\(R\\)-algebra surjection\n\\[\nB_F\\twoheadrightarrow H_F^*(X).\n\\tag{4.1}\n\\]\nEquivalently,\n\\[\n\\ker(R\\to B_F)\\subseteq\\ker(R\\to H_F^*(X)).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The assertion is immediate if \\(D=\\varnothing\\). Otherwise let \\(u\\in R\\) be homogeneous and zero in \\(B_F\\). Proposition 1 expresses \\(u\\) as a finite sum of positive-length words in nontrivial dressed positive and negative minuscule generators. Retaining topological charge zero makes every word neutral.</p><p>The character-line degrees of the steps sum to zero. Choose a step with nonpositive character-line degree and put it first by commutativity. Its topological charge is nonzero. Apply Lemma 2.1 with a common neutral endpoint bound \\(Z\\). Each summand of \\(u\\rho_Z\\) factors through its first-step object, with the prescribed shift. Lemma 2.3 annihilates every such object, so\n\\[\n\\mathsf T(u\\rho_Z)=0.\n\\]</p><p>The degree calculation in Lemma 2.1 includes all nonneutral intermediate ranks. Lemma 2.4 gives the same quadratic shift on source and target. By \\(R\\)-linearity,\n\\[\n(u|_D)\\smile j^*=0.\n\\]\nApply this to \\(\\pi^*(1)\\); since \\(j^*\\pi^*(1)=1\\), one obtains\n\\[\nu|_D=0\\quad\\text{in }H_K^*(D).\n\\]\nFreeness identifies \\(H_K^*(D)=H_F^*(X)\\), compatibly with coefficients. Therefore\n\\[\n\\ker(R\\to B_F)\\subseteq\\ker(R\\to H_F^*(X)).\n\\]\nBoth coefficient maps are surjective by Proposition 1, giving (4.1).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.1", "orig": "Lemma 3.1", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For any chosen ordering of each word, there are a neutral Schubert bound \\(Z\\) and a common truncation such that …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Put \\(K=G\\times F\\). All homology in this lemma is equivariant Borel–Moore homology.</p><p class=\"li\">A point \\(b=gG(\\mathcal O)\\in\\operatorname{Gr}_G\\) determines \\(gN(\\mathcal O)\\). In a BFN triple use \\(n=gs_0\\), whose fiber is \\(N(\\mathcal O)\\cap gN(\\mathcal O)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For a projective \\(K\\)-variety \\(B\\) with a \\(K\\)-equivariant bounded map to \\(\\operatorname{Gr}_G\\), choose \\(l\\) so all lattices contain \\(t^lN(\\mathcal O)\\) and lie in \\(t^{-l}N(\\mathcal O)\\). Set\n  \\[\n  L=t^{-l}N(\\mathcal O)/t^lN(\\mathcal O),\\qquad\n  E_b=gN(\\mathcal O)/t^lN(\\mathcal O),\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  S_B=\\{(b,n)\\in B\\times L:n\\in E_b\\},\\qquad\n  f_B:S_B\\to L,\\qquad\n  \\mathcal F_B=Rf_{B,*}\\mathbb C_{S_B}.\n  \\]\n  These are section bundles, considered componentwise if ranks vary, and \\(f_B\\) is proper.</p><p class=\"li\">With \\(\\chi_N=\\det(N|_G)\\), the rank on topological component \\(\\gamma\\) is\n  \\[\n  r_B=l\\dim N-\\langle\\chi_N,\\gamma\\rangle.\n  \\tag{3.2}\n  \\]\n  Put \\(r=l\\dim N\\), \\(S_{\\mathrm{pt}}=N(\\mathcal O)/t^lN(\\mathcal O)\\), and \\(i=f_{\\mathrm{pt}}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(Z\\) be a finite union of affine Schubert varieties in the neutral component, containing the identity lattice. Restriction gives\n  \\[\n  \\rho_Z:\\mathcal F_Z\\to\\mathcal F_{\\mathrm{pt}}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(u\\in R\\) be homogeneous of degree \\(d\\), with an equality in \\(A_F\\)\n  \\[\n  u=\\sum_w\n      M_{\\lambda_{w,1},p_{w,1}}\\cdots\n      M_{\\lambda_{w,k_w},p_{w,k_w}},\n  \\qquad\n  k_w\\ge1,\\qquad\n  \\sum_j[\\lambda_{w,j}]=0.\n  \\tag{3.4}\n  \\]\n  Each factor is a nontrivial dressed positive or negative minuscule generator, and every word is homogeneous of degree \\(d\\). Coefficients may be absorbed in dressings.</p><p class=\"li\">Write\n  \\[\n  \\operatorname{Hom}_{K,L}^a(\\mathcal F,\\mathcal G)\n  =\\operatorname{Hom}(\\mathcal F,\\mathcal G[a]).\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For any chosen ordering of each word, there are a neutral Schubert bound \\(Z\\) and a common truncation such that\n\\[\nu\\rho_Z=\\sum_w\\Psi_w\n\\quad\\text{in}\\quad\n\\operatorname{Hom}_{K,L}^d(\\mathcal F_Z,\\mathcal F_{\\mathrm{pt}}).\n\\tag{3.5}\n\\]\nEach \\(\\Psi_w\\) factors through a cohomological shift of\n\\[\n\\mathcal F_{\\operatorname{Gr}_G^{\\lambda_{w,1}}}.\n\\]\nOnly the endpoints must lie in \\(Z\\) or in the neutral component; the common truncation includes all intermediate lattices.</p>", "proof": "<p>For proper maps \\(f:X\\to L\\), \\(g:Y\\to L\\), proper base change and adjunction give\n\\[\nR\\operatorname{Hom}_L(Rf_*\\mathbb C_X,Rg_*\\omega_Y)\n\\simeq\nR\\Gamma(X\\times_LY,\\omega_{X\\times_LY}).\n\\]\nIndeed, with \\(q:X\\times_LY\\to Y\\), the left side becomes\n\\[\nR\\operatorname{Hom}_Y(q_*\\mathbb C_{X\\times_LY},\\omega_Y),\n\\]\nand \\(q_*\\dashv q^!\\) gives the identity. If \\(Y\\) is smooth of complex dimension \\(e\\), then\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Hom}_{K,L}^a(Rf_*\\mathbb C_X,Rg_*\\mathbb C_Y)\n\\simeq H_{2e-a}^K(X\\times_LY).\n\\tag{3.6}\n\\]\nUse common Borel approximations; their additional dimensions cancel. The proper-support base-change and adjunction formalism is [38, Theorems 5.10 and 7.7], and duality and Borel–Moore identifications are [31, Example 2.7(3) and Corollary 2.11]. The source need not be smooth. Composition across a smooth middle space is refined intersection along its diagonal followed by proper pushforward.</p><p>Fix one word and suppress \\(w\\). Let \\(B_j\\) parametrize chains beginning at the identity with successive relative positions \\(\\lambda_1,\\ldots,\\lambda_j\\), and let \\(B_0=\\mathrm{pt}\\). The maps \\(B_j\\to B_{j-1}\\) are bundles with smooth closed minuscule fibers. Thus \\(B_j\\) is smooth and projective. Put\n\\[\na_j=\\dim\\operatorname{Gr}_G^{\\lambda_j},\\qquad\nr_j=\\operatorname{rk}E_{b_j},\n\\]\n\\[\nI_j=\\{(b_0,\\ldots,b_j,n):n\\in E_{b_{j-1}}\\cap E_{b_j}\\}.\n\\]\nFixed relative position makes this intersection a vector bundle. It gives\n\\[\nS_{B_j}\\xleftarrow{v_j}I_j\\xrightarrow{u_j}S_{B_{j-1}},\n\\]\nwhere \\(v_j\\) is a closed subbundle inclusion and \\(u_j\\) forgets the last lattice. Write\n\\[\nc_j=\\operatorname{rk}(E_{b_j}/I_j).\n\\]\nThe dressing \\(p_j\\) defines a characteristic class on \\(B_j\\), using the two tautological blocks in the preceding lattice frame and the flavor classes.</p><p>If \\(\\delta_j=\\deg p_j\\), the class \\(p_j\\cap[I_j]\\) gives a morphism\n\\[\n\\mathcal F_{B_j}\\to\\mathcal F_{B_{j-1}}[q_j],\n\\qquad\nq_j=2(r_{j-1}-r_j+c_j-a_j)+\\delta_j.\n\\tag{3.7}\n\\]\nThis follows from\n\\[\n\\dim I_j=\\dim B_{j-1}+a_j+r_j-c_j\n\\]\nand (3.6), with target dimension \\(\\dim B_{j-1}+r_{j-1}\\).</p><p>Choose \\(Z\\) bounding the endpoint images. The endpoint map \\(S_{B_k}\\to S_Z\\) is proper; the unit on constant sheaves gives \\(\\mathcal F_Z\\to\\mathcal F_{B_k}\\) with no shift. Composing with the incidence morphisms defines \\(\\Psi_w\\), with the asserted first-step factorization.</p><p>For a modification of type \\(\\lambda_j\\),\n\\[\nc_j=\\sum_{\\beta}(-\\langle\\beta,\\lambda_j\\rangle)_+.\n\\]\nBy Proposition 1, its rational coefficient has degree \\(2c_j-2a_j+\\delta_j\\). Neutrality gives \\(r_k=r_0=r\\), so\n\\[\n\\sum_jq_j\n=2(r_0-r_k)+\\sum_j(2c_j-2a_j+\\delta_j)=d.\n\\]\nThus both sides of (3.5) lie in the same graded Hom space.</p><p>To establish a faithful localization test, set\n\\[\nC_Z=S_{\\mathrm{pt}}\\times_LS_Z,\\qquad\n\\iota:C_Z\\hookrightarrow S_Z.\n\\]\nEquation (3.6) identifies the relevant Hom group with \\(H_{2r-d}^K(C_Z)\\); \\(\\rho_Z\\) is the fundamental class of \\(S_{\\mathrm{pt}}\\) over the identity lattice.</p><p>Put \\(P=H_{T\\times F}^*(\\mathrm{pt})\\). Over each of the finitely many \\(G(\\mathcal O)\\)-orbits in \\(Z\\), the space \\(C_Z\\) is a vector bundle. The orbit is an affine-space bundle over a partial flag variety and contracts to it under \\(t\\mapsto at\\), \\(a\\to0\\), commuting with \\(T\\); \\(F\\) acts trivially on the orbit. Its equivariant cohomology is even and free over \\(P\\). Smooth equivariant Poincaré duality and the section-bundle Thom shift give the same properties for the corresponding Borel–Moore stratum. Closed-stratum sequences are short exact by parity, with free quotients. Hence\n\\[\nH_*^{T\\times F}(C_Z)\n\\]\nis free over \\(P\\), in particular torsion-free.</p><p>The \\(T\\)-fixed lattices are the diagonal lattices \\(t^\\eta\\). At \\(t^\\eta\\), any \\((T\\times F)\\)-weight-zero summand of \\(N\\) pairs trivially with \\(\\eta\\), so intersection with \\(N(\\mathcal O)\\) does not change its lattice. Therefore \\(\\iota\\) identifies fixed loci. Localization makes \\(\\iota_*\\) an isomorphism over \\(\\operatorname{Frac}P\\), and torsion-freeness makes localized pushforward faithful. Weyl invariants recover \\(K\\)-equivariance.</p><p>After localization, the entire section-fiber classes \\([E_{t^\\eta}]\\), formally denoted \\(e^\\eta\\), form a basis for the homology of \\(S_Z\\). Using whole fibers avoids inverting zero Euler classes from weight-zero summands.</p><p>For each partial chain set\n\\[\nC_j=S_{\\mathrm{pt}}\\times_LS_{B_j},\\qquad\nh_j:C_j\\hookrightarrow S_{B_j}.\n\\]\nLet \\(z_j\\) be the Borel–Moore class of the first \\(j\\) incidence morphisms. Since \\(S_{B_j}\\) is smooth, write\n\\[\n(h_j)_*z_j=\\alpha_j\\cap[S_{B_j}].\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\n\\alpha_k=\\prod_{j=1}^kp_j\\,\\operatorname{eu}(E_{b_j}/I_j).\n\\tag{3.8}\n\\]\nIndeed, \\(\\alpha_0=1\\). Refined intersection along the smooth middle diagonal commutes with forgetting the initial section constraint, and at step \\(j\\) gives\n\\[\n(h_j)_*z_j\n=(v_j)_*\\bigl(u_j^*\\alpha_{j-1}\\,p_j\\cap[I_j]\\bigr).\n\\]\nThe subbundle inclusion multiplies by \\(\\operatorname{eu}(E_{b_j}/I_j)\\), proving (3.8). No smoothness of \\(u_j\\) is required.</p><p>Under the Hom–Borel–Moore identification, precomposition with the endpoint unit is proper pushforward along \\(C_k\\to C_Z\\). Forgetting the initial section constraint commutes with this pushforward. Thus endpoint pushforward of (3.8) is the localized image of \\(\\Psi_w\\).</p><p>Fixed chains are sequences of Weyl translates \\(\\lambda_j'\\), with successive lattices\n\\[\nt^{\\eta_j},\\qquad \\eta_j=\\sum_{h\\le j}\\lambda_h'.\n\\]\nTranslation by \\(t^{\\eta_{j-1}}\\) does not change weights or dressings, since there is no loop rotation. Localization on the smooth chain base therefore sends the word to\n\\[\n\\sum_{(\\lambda_1',\\ldots,\\lambda_k')}\ne^{\\lambda_1'+\\cdots+\\lambda_k'}\n\\prod_{j=1}^k\n\\left(\np_{j,\\lambda_j'}\n\\frac{\\prod_\\beta\\beta^{(-\\langle\\beta,\\lambda_j'\\rangle)_+}}\n{\\operatorname{eu}_T(T_{t^{\\lambda_j'}}\\operatorname{Gr}_G^{\\lambda_j})}\n\\right).\n\\]\nThis is the product of the abelianized expressions (2.2). Endpoint ranks equal \\(r\\), so there is no extra fiber factor. The localized pushforward of \\(u\\rho_Z\\) is \\(ue^0\\). The assumed equality (3.4), followed by abelianization and the faithful localization test, proves (3.5).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.2", "orig": "Lemma 4.2", "depth": 1, "snippet": "One has \\(\\deg L_\\nu\\ge0\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(P\\) be a principal \\(G\\)-bundle on \\(\\mathbb P^1\\), framed at infinity.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\sigma\\) be a global section of \\(P\\times_G T^*N\\), whose framed value \\(\\sigma_\\infty\\) is \\(\\nu\\)-stable with trivial stabilizer.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(L_\\nu\\) be the character line bundle, with the convention that evaluating a \\(\\nu^m\\)-semi-invariant gives a section of \\(L_\\nu^{\\otimes m}\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>One has \\(\\deg L_\\nu\\ge0\\). If \\(\\deg L_\\nu=0\\), there is a unique trivialization agreeing with the frame at infinity in which \\(\\sigma\\) is the constant section \\(\\sigma_\\infty\\).</p><p>If \\(P\\) is specified by \\(b\\in\\operatorname{Gr}_G\\), using the usual trivialization on \\(\\mathbb P^1\\setminus\\{0\\}\\), then in the degree-zero case the topological class is zero and\n\\[\nb=hG(\\mathcal O),\\qquad\nh\\in G(\\mathbb C[t^{-1}]),\\qquad h(\\infty)=1.\n\\tag{4.2}\n\\]\nIn the off-zero frame, \\(\\sigma=h\\sigma_\\infty\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose a \\(\\nu^m\\)-semi-invariant, \\(m&gt;0\\), nonzero at \\(\\sigma_\\infty\\). Its evaluation is a global section of a positive power of \\(L_\\nu\\), nonzero at infinity, proving nonnegativity.</p><p>If the degree is zero, that section is nowhere zero. Hence \\(\\sigma\\) is everywhere semistable and determines\n\\[\n\\gamma:\\mathbb P^1\\to(T^*N)//_\\nu G.\n\\]\nEvery invariant polynomial evaluated on \\(\\sigma\\) is constant on \\(\\mathbb P^1\\), so the image lies in one fiber of the projective morphism to \\((T^*N)//G\\). A sufficiently divisible GIT polarization is relatively ample and pulls back to a positive power of \\(L_\\nu\\). Its degree is zero, so \\(\\gamma\\) is constant [27, Section 2].</p><p>The constant value is the stable quotient point of \\(\\sigma_\\infty\\), whose semistable fiber is the single orbit \\(G\\sigma_\\infty\\). Thus \\(\\sigma\\) is a section of the associated orbit bundle. Since \\(G\\sigma_\\infty\\cong G\\) equivariantly, this section trivializes \\(P\\). Freeness gives uniqueness with the prescribed coordinates and frame.</p><p>Comparing this global trivialization with the off-zero trivialization gives a regular map \\(\\mathbb A^1_{t^{-1}}\\to G\\), equal to \\(1\\) at infinity. This is \\(h\\), and the lattice of regular sections at zero is \\(hG(\\mathcal O)\\).</p><p>For later use, every point of \\(D\\) is stable with trivial stabilizer in the ambient representation \\(T^*N\\): semi-invariants lift from a closed invariant subvariety by exactness of reductive invariants, and orbit closures stay in that subvariety.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.3", "orig": "Lemma 4.3", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The test is \\(R\\)-linear, and \\( \\mathsf T(\\mathcal F_B) \\simeq R\\Gamma_K(M_B,\\Phi_{W_B}\\mathbb C_{M_B}). \\) The support of \\(\\Phi_{W_B}\\mathbb C_{M_B}\\) lies in the closed locus \\(\\Sigma_B\\) defined by …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Retain \\(K,L,E_b,S_B,f_B,\\mathcal F_B\\), and \\(l\\) from Lemma 2.1, and the stable zero-moment locus \\(D\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Decompose\n  \\[\n  L_-=\\bigoplus_{j=-l}^{-1}t^jN,\\qquad\n  L_+=\\bigoplus_{j=0}^{l-1}t^jN,\\qquad\n  P_-=\\bigoplus_{j=-l}^{-1}t^jN^*.\n  \\]\n  Then \\(L=L_-\\oplus L_+\\), and the residue pairing identifies \\(P_-=L_+^*\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Regard \\(s\\in N\\) and \\(p\\in N^*\\) as constant series. On\n  \\[\n  \\widetilde L=L\\times D\\times P_-\n  \\]\n  define\n  \\[\n  W(n,s,p,y)=\n  \\operatorname{Res}\\bigl(\\langle p+y,n\\rangle-\\langle y,s\\rangle\\bigr),\n  \\tag{4.3}\n  \\]\n  where \\(\\operatorname{Res}\\) is the coefficient of \\(t^{-1}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Use shifted vanishing cycles \\(\\Phi_f=\\varphi_f[-1]\\), normalized by \\(\\Phi_0=\\mathrm{id}\\), and regard them on the ambient space by closed direct image from the zero fiber.</p><p class=\"li\">Define the test\n  \\[\n  \\mathsf T(\\mathcal F)\n  =R\\Gamma_K(\\widetilde L,\\Phi_W\\operatorname{pr}_L^*\\mathcal F).\n  \\tag{4.4}\n  \\]\n  For \\(M_B=S_B\\times D\\times P_-\\), let \\(W_B\\) be the pulled-back function.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The test is \\(R\\)-linear, and\n\\[\n\\mathsf T(\\mathcal F_B)\n\\simeq R\\Gamma_K(M_B,\\Phi_{W_B}\\mathbb C_{M_B}).\n\\tag{4.5}\n\\]\nThe support of \\(\\Phi_{W_B}\\mathbb C_{M_B}\\) lies in the closed locus \\(\\Sigma_B\\) defined by\n\\[\nn\\in s+L_-,\n\\qquad\np+y\\in gN^*(\\mathcal O)/t^lN^*(\\mathcal O),\n\\qquad b=gG(\\mathcal O).\n\\tag{4.6}\n\\]\nIf \\(B\\) lies in a nonzero topological component with nonpositive character-line degree, then \\(\\Sigma_B=\\varnothing\\), and the test vanishes.</p>", "proof": "<p>Proper base change and compatibility of vanishing cycles with proper direct image give (4.5); use [31, Proposition 4.19(1)], with smooth projection pullback from part (2).</p><p>For \\(R\\)-linearity, use a finite Borel approximation with base \\(BK\\). A coefficient \\(u\\) is a morphism\n\\[\n\\mathbb C_{BK}\\to\\mathbb C_{BK}[\\deg u]\n\\]\npulled back to each space. The projection formula makes proper direct image compatible with this action [38, Theorem 6.3]. Nearby cycles commute naturally with tensoring by locally constant complexes pulled back from the base. Taking the specialization cone gives the same compatibility for vanishing cycles. Naturality identifies the tested action with cup product by the restricted coefficient.</p><p>Differentiating in \\(y\\) gives \\(\\operatorname{pr}_{L_+}n=s\\). Differentiating in \\(n\\in E_b\\) says that \\(p+y\\) annihilates \\(E_b\\) under the residue pairing. Its annihilator is the displayed truncated dual lattice. These are zeros of algebraic bundle maps and define a closed locus.</p><p>This support bound holds even when \\(B\\) is singular. Off \\(\\Sigma_B\\), a derivative in a vector-bundle fiber coordinate is nonzero. The function is affine linear in that coordinate; where its coefficient is invertible, replacing that coordinate by \\(W_B\\) gives a product chart over the unchanged base. The constant sheaf has zero vanishing cycles there.</p><p>A bounded lattice gives a modification at zero of the trivial principal bundle on \\(\\mathbb P^1\\), determined by finite jets. A point of \\(\\Sigma_B\\) gives global cotangent sections whose off-zero expressions are \\(n\\) and \\(p+y\\), polynomials in \\(t^{-1}\\) with values \\(s,p\\) at infinity. The lattice conditions are exactly extension across zero; discarded \\(t^l\\)-tails lie in the corresponding lattices.</p><p>Lemma 2.2 excludes negative character-line degree and forces the entire topological class to be zero in degree zero. Hence \\(\\Sigma_B\\) is empty in the stated nonzero components.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.4", "orig": "Lemma 4.4", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The locus \\(\\Sigma_Z\\) is a closed graph \\(\\Gamma\\cong U\\times D\\) in \\(M_Z\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Retain the truncation and test of Lemmas 2.1 and 2.3.</p><p class=\"li\">Assume \\(D\\ne\\varnothing\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(Z\\) be the neutral projective Schubert union containing the identity lattice. All \\(E_b\\), \\(b\\in Z\\), have rank \\(r=l\\dim N\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  U=\\{b\\in Z:\\operatorname{pr}_{L_+}:E_b\\xrightarrow{\\sim}L_+\\}.\n  \\tag{4.7}\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The locus \\(\\Sigma_Z\\) is a closed graph \\(\\Gamma\\cong U\\times D\\) in \\(M_Z\\). There are compatible \\(K\\)-equivariant identifications\n\\[\n\\Phi_{W_Z}\\mathbb C_{M_Z}\n\\simeq\\iota_{\\Gamma,*}\\mathbb C_\\Gamma[-2r],\n\\qquad\n\\Phi_{W_{\\mathrm{pt}}}\\mathbb C_{M_{\\mathrm{pt}}}\n\\simeq\\iota_{D,*}\\mathbb C_D[-2r].\n\\tag{4.8}\n\\]\nUnder these identifications, the test of \\(\\rho_Z\\) is ordinary restriction\n\\[\nH_K^*(U\\times D)\\to H_K^*(D),\n\\tag{4.9}\n\\]\nwith the same uniform shift on source and target. It is split surjective and \\(R\\)-linear.</p>", "proof": "<p>Every point of \\(\\Sigma_Z\\) lies over \\(U\\). Lemma 2.2 supplies a based loop \\(h\\) trivializing its principal bundle. An element of the kernel \\(E_b\\to L_+\\) is a negative polynomial section of its associated \\(N\\)-bundle, extending at zero and vanishing at infinity. The associated bundle is trivial, so this section vanishes. Equal dimensions make the projection an isomorphism.</p><p>Over \\(U\\), write \\(E_b\\) as the graph of a regular map\n\\[\nA_b:L_+\\to L_-,\n\\qquad n=a+A_ba.\n\\]\nDefine\n\\[\n\\ell_{b,p}(a)=\\operatorname{Res}\\langle p,A_ba\\rangle.\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\nW_Z=\\langle y,a-s\\rangle+\\ell_{b,p}(a).\n\\tag{4.10}\n\\]\nIts fiber critical equations have the unique solution\n\\[\na=s,\\qquad y=-\\ell_{b,p},\n\\]\nregular in \\((b,s,p)\\). Thus \\(\\Sigma_Z\\) is the graph \\(\\Gamma\\cong U\\times D\\). It is closed in all of \\(M_Z\\), since its defining equations are closed and every solution lies over \\(U\\).</p><p>At this solution, Lemma 2.2 gives\n\\[\nn=hs,\\qquad p+y=hp.\n\\]\nInvariance of the pairing gives \\(\\langle hp,hs\\rangle=\\langle p,s\\rangle\\), so\n\\[\nW_{\\mathrm{crit}}=-\\langle[t^{-1}](hp),s\\rangle.\n\\]\nWrite \\(h=1+\\xi t^{-1}+O(t^{-2})\\), with \\(\\xi\\in\\mathfrak g\\). Then\n\\[\nW_{\\mathrm{crit}}\n=-\\langle\\xi\\cdot p,s\\rangle\n=\\langle p,\\xi\\cdot s\\rangle\n=\\langle\\mu(s,p),\\xi\\rangle=0.\n\\tag{4.11}\n\\]\nThe regular equivariant translations\n\\[\na'=a-s,\\qquad y'=y+\\ell_{b,p}\n\\]\ntherefore turn \\(W_Z\\) into the fixed split form \\(\\langle y',a'\\rangle\\).</p><p>The vanishing cycles of this form on \\(L_+\\oplus L_+^*\\) are one copy of \\(\\mathbb C[-2r]\\) at zero. The external-product theorem applies to the constant sheaf of \\(U\\times D\\), even if \\(U\\) is singular [30]. The splitting is global and equivariant; its structure group acts as \\(g\\oplus g^{-T}\\), so the quadratic vanishing-cycle line has trivial equivariant monodromy. One choice of generator gives compatible identifications throughout.</p><p>Lemma 2.3 gives zero vanishing cycles off the closed graph, including over \\(Z\\setminus U\\), proving (4.8). At the identity lattice, \\(A_1=0\\), \\(\\ell_{1,p}=0\\), and the same \\(2r\\) quadratic variables occur.</p><p>For the morphism, set\n\\[\nj:D\\to U\\times D,\\quad j(d)=(1,d),\n\\qquad\n\\pi:U\\times D\\to D,\\quad\\pi(b,d)=d.\n\\]\nBoth maps are \\(K\\)-equivariant and \\(\\pi j=\\mathrm{id}\\). Apply the natural external-product identification to\n\\[\n\\mathbb C_{U\\times D}\\to Rj_*\\mathbb C_D\n\\]\nwith zero function on the base and the fixed split quadratic form. Since \\(j\\) is closed, proper-direct-image compatibility identifies the target with the quadratic calculation over \\(D\\). A common quadratic generator identifies the tested morphism with\n\\[\n\\mathbb C_{U\\times D}[-2r]\\to Rj_*\\mathbb C_D[-2r].\n\\]\nTaking ordinary equivariant cohomology gives \\(j^*\\), and\n\\[\nj^*\\pi^*=\\mathrm{id}\n\\]\nprovides the \\(R\\)-linear splitting. No properness of \\(\\pi\\) is required.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 3", "orig": "Proposition 5.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Under the abelianization embedding of Proposition 1, \\( A_0= \\left(\\bigoplus_{\\lambda\\in\\mathbb Z^\\Omega}Pr_\\lambda\\right)^W. \\) This identification preserves \\(R_0=P^W\\) and topological charge.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Unlike the general setting of Theorem 1, flavor is now trivial, and the quiver has at least one loop at every vertex of positive gauge dimension.</p><p class=\"li\">Delete zero-rank vertices and choose one loop at each remaining vertex. Let \\(Q'\\) be obtained by deleting these chosen loops.</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  n_{ij}=\\#Q'_1(i,j),\\qquad\n  c_{ij}=n_{ij}+n_{ji},\\qquad\n  C(v)=\\frac12\\sum_{i,j}c_{ij}v_iv_j.\n  \\]\n  Thus \\(c_{ii}=2n_{ii}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let\n  \\[\n  \\Omega=\\{(i,a):1\\le a\\le v_i\\},\\quad\n  P=\\mathbb C[x_{ia}:(i,a)\\in\\Omega],\\quad\n  W=\\prod_iS_{v_i}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mathcal B'\\) be the multiset of weights \\(x_{jb}-x_{ia}\\) of arrows of \\(Q'\\), together with \\(w_i\\) copies of \\(-x_{ia}\\) at each slot. Omit zero weights from diagonal loop entries.</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(\\lambda\\in\\mathbb Z^\\Omega\\), define\n  \\[\n  E_\\beta(\\lambda)=(-\\langle\\beta,\\lambda\\rangle)_+,\\qquad\n  r_\\lambda=e^\\lambda\\prod_{\\beta\\in\\mathcal B'}\\beta^{E_\\beta(\\lambda)}\n  \\in\\mathbb C(x_{ia})[\\mathbb Z^\\Omega].\n  \\]\n  For \\(p\\in P^{W_\\lambda}\\), put\n  \\[\n  S_\\lambda(p)=\\sum_{w\\in W/W_\\lambda}w(pr_\\lambda),\n  \\]\n  with each distinct translate occurring once.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Under the abelianization embedding of Proposition 1,\n\\[\nA_0=\n\\left(\\bigoplus_{\\lambda\\in\\mathbb Z^\\Omega}Pr_\\lambda\\right)^W.\n\\tag{5.1}\n\\]\nThis identification preserves \\(R_0=P^W\\) and topological charge.</p>", "proof": "<p>The right side is an algebra, since\n\\[\nr_\\alpha r_\\gamma\n=r_{\\alpha+\\gamma}\n \\prod_{\\beta\\in\\mathcal B'}\n \\beta^{E_\\beta(\\alpha)+E_\\beta(\\gamma)-E_\\beta(\\alpha+\\gamma)},\n\\tag{5.2}\n\\]\nand every exponent is nonnegative. The multiset \\(\\mathcal B'\\) is \\(W\\)-stable.</p><p>For a positive or negative subset coweight, the deleted loop weights cancel the tangent Euler denominator in (2.2), up to a nonzero constant sign. For a positive subset \\(I\\), the loop contributes\n\\[\n\\prod_{a\\in I,\\ b\\notin I}(x_{ib}-x_{ia});\n\\]\nthese are the Grassmannian tangent roots up to the common sign convention. For a negative subset the roots are reversed; compare [42, Lemma 2.10]. Proposition 1 places all generators of \\(A_0\\) in the right side of (5.1), and places every subset-coweight orbit sum \\(S_\\alpha(p)\\) in \\(A_0\\).</p><p>To prove the reverse inclusion, induct strongly on\n\\[\n\\|\\lambda\\|^2=\\sum_{(i,a)\\in\\Omega}\\lambda_{ia}^2.\n\\]\nThe coefficient and positive or negative subset cases are established. If \\(\\lambda\\) has a positive coordinate, set\n\\[\nf(t)=1_{t&gt;0},\\qquad g(t)=t-1_{t&gt;0};\n\\]\notherwise set\n\\[\nf(t)=-1_{t&lt;0},\\qquad g(t)=t+1_{t&lt;0}.\n\\]\nDefine \\(\\alpha=f(\\lambda)\\), \\(\\gamma=g(\\lambda)\\) coordinatewise. Outside the base cases, both have smaller squared norm. The functions are nondecreasing and have compatible signs relative to zero. Consequently\n\\[\n(\\alpha_d-\\alpha_e)(\\gamma_d-\\gamma_e)\\ge0,\n\\qquad\n\\alpha_d\\gamma_d\\ge0\n\\]\nfor all slots, including different colors. Every exponent in (5.2) is therefore zero, so \\(r_\\alpha r_\\gamma=r_\\lambda\\).</p><p>Order slots at each vertex by their \\(\\lambda\\)-coordinates. Then \\(\\alpha,\\gamma\\) are similarly ordered. The rearrangement inequality gives\n\\[\n\\|\\alpha+w\\gamma\\|^2\\le\\|\\alpha+\\gamma\\|^2=\\|\\lambda\\|^2.\n\\]\nIn the equality case the sum belongs to the orbit of \\(\\lambda\\). Sorting a matching increases its scalar product strictly unless one corresponding pair is tied; swaps involving ties leave the multiset of sums unchanged. Hence all maximal matchings give the same orbit, and all other terms in\n\\[\nS_\\alpha(p_\\alpha)S_\\gamma(p_\\gamma)\n\\]\nhave strictly smaller squared norm.</p><p>At the fixed representative \\(\\lambda\\), the decomposition into translates of \\(\\alpha,\\gamma\\) is unique. In the positive case, a different assignment would put a \\(1\\) at a coordinate \\(\\lambda_d\\le0\\) and a \\(0\\) at a coordinate \\(\\lambda_e&gt;0\\) of the same color. The corresponding \\(\\gamma\\)-entries satisfy\n\\[\n\\gamma'_d=\\lambda_d-1&lt;\\lambda_e=\\gamma'_e,\n\\]\na strict inversion relative to \\(\\alpha'\\), contradicting maximality. In the negative case, assigning \\(0\\) to a negative coordinate and \\(-1\\) to a nonnegative coordinate gives\n\\[\n\\gamma'_d=\\lambda_d&lt;\\lambda_e+1=\\gamma'_e,\n\\]\nagain a strict inversion. Thus the coefficient of \\(r_\\lambda\\) is exactly \\(p_\\alpha p_\\gamma\\).</p><p>The level blocks of \\(\\alpha,\\gamma\\) are intervals in the chosen ordering, and their combined cuts are exactly the level-block cuts of \\(\\lambda\\), since \\(f(t)+g(t)=t\\). For each \\(k\\ge1\\), power sums on any \\(\\lambda\\)-block are linear combinations of power sums on \\(\\alpha\\)- and \\(\\gamma\\)-blocks: obtain prefix sums at each cut and subtract successive prefixes. Power sums generate symmetric-polynomial rings over \\(\\mathbb C\\), hence\n\\[\nP^{W_\\lambda}\n=\\langle P^{W_\\alpha},P^{W_\\gamma}\\rangle.\n\\]\nEvery dressing is therefore a finite sum of products \\(p_\\alpha p_\\gamma\\), realizing arbitrary leading coefficients.</p><p>All other terms have polynomial dressings by (5.2), invariant under their coweight stabilizers because the product is \\(W\\)-invariant. The induction hypothesis removes them. This proves the reverse inclusion without inverting any polynomial.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 4", "orig": "Proposition 5.2", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The full-topological-torus quotient satisfies the coefficientwise bound \\( \\operatorname{Hilb}(B_0;q) \\le \\sum_{m\\in\\mathcal M(v)} q^{C(v)-D_m+w\\cdot(v-l)}h_m(q). \\) Every displayed shift is a nonnegative integer.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Retain the looped, no-flavor setting and \\(c_{ij},C(v),P,W,r_\\lambda,S_\\lambda\\) of Proposition 3.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(q\\) record half cohomological degree, so each polynomial variable has degree one.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mathcal M(v)\\) consist of arrays\n  \\[\n  m=(m_{i,s})_{i\\in Q_0,s\\ge1},\\qquad\n  m_{i,s}\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0},\\qquad\n  \\sum_{s\\ge1}sm_{i,s}=v_i.\n  \\]\n  Set\n  \\[\n  l_i=\\sum_sm_{i,s},\\qquad l=(l_i)_i,\\qquad |l|=\\sum_il_i,\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  D_m=\\frac12\\sum_{i,j}\\sum_{s,t\\ge1}\n      c_{ij}\\min(s,t)m_{i,s}m_{j,t}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\Omega_m^+\\) consist of \\(m_{i,s}\\) distinguished slots of type \\((i,s)\\). Write \\(i(d),s(d)\\) for the color and size of a slot \\(d\\). Put\n  \\[\n  P_m=\\mathbb C[x_d:d\\in\\Omega_m^+],\\qquad\n  W_m=\\prod_{i,s}S_{m_{i,s}},\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  b_{de}=c_{i(d)i(e)}\\min(s(d),s(e)).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For each nonempty \\(S\\subseteq\\Omega_m^+\\), define\n  \\[\n  q_S=\n  \\prod_{d\\in S}x_d^{w_{i(d)}}\n  \\prod_{\\substack{d\\in S\\\\e\\in\\Omega_m^+\\setminus S}}\n  (x_d-x_e)^{b_{de}}.\n  \\tag{5.3}\n  \\]\n  Let \\(J_m=(q_S:\\varnothing\\ne S\\subseteq\\Omega_m^+)\\), and set\n  \\[\n  h_m(q)=\\operatorname{Hilb}((P_m/J_m)^{W_m};q).\n  \\]\n  For the empty array, \\(P_m=\\mathbb C\\), \\(J_m=0\\), \\(h_m=1\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The full-topological-torus quotient satisfies the coefficientwise bound\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Hilb}(B_0;q)\n\\le\n\\sum_{m\\in\\mathcal M(v)}\nq^{C(v)-D_m+w\\cdot(v-l)}h_m(q).\n\\tag{5.4}\n\\]\nEvery displayed shift is a nonnegative integer.</p>", "proof": "<p>For each color, identify its \\(l_i\\) positive slots with \\(l_i\\) of the \\(v_i\\) gauge slots. Give all remaining slots size zero, and denote them by \\(\\Omega_m^0\\). Put\n\\[\n(\\lambda_m)_d=s(d)-1.\n\\]\nSince the sizes at vertex \\(i\\) sum to \\(v_i\\), \\([\\lambda_m]=0\\), and\n\\[\nW_{\\lambda_m}=W_m\\times\\prod_iS_{v_i-l_i}.\n\\]</p><p>The half degree of \\(r_{\\lambda_m}\\) is its coefficient’s polynomial degree. For an arrow \\(i\\to j\\) of \\(Q'\\), its contribution is\n\\[\n\\sum_{a=1}^{v_i}\\sum_{b=1}^{v_j}\n\\max(s(ia)-s(jb),0)\n=\nv_iv_j-\\sum_{a,b}\\min(s(ia),s(jb)).\n\\]\nThe framing contribution at \\(i\\) is\n\\[\nw_i\\sum_{\\substack{d\\in\\Omega_m^+\\\\i(d)=i}}(s(d)-1)\n=w_i(v_i-l_i).\n\\]\nHence\n\\[\n\\delta_m:=\\deg_qr_{\\lambda_m}\n=C(v)-D_m+w\\cdot(v-l).\n\\tag{5.5}\n\\]\nThis proves integrality and nonnegativity, including remaining loops.</p><p>For \\(-1\\le L\\le\\sum_iv_i\\), set \\(F_{-1}=0\\), and let \\(F_L\\subseteq B_0\\) be the sum of images\n\\[\nS_{\\lambda_m}(P^{W_{\\lambda_m}}),\\qquad |l|\\le L.\n\\]\nThe array \\(m_{i,1}=v_i\\) has \\(\\lambda_m=0\\), so the last stage contains every coefficient. Proposition 1 therefore gives \\(F_{\\sum_iv_i}=B_0\\). Define\n\\[\n\\Phi_m:P^{W_{\\lambda_m}}\\to F_{|l|}/F_{|l|-1},\n\\qquad p\\mapsto[S_{\\lambda_m}(p)].\n\\]\nIt adds shift \\(\\delta_m\\).</p><p>First eliminate zero-slot dressings. For each \\(i,k\\ge1\\), define a derivation on the ambient Laurent ring by\n\\[\nD_{i,k}(pe^\\lambda)\n=pe^\\lambda\\sum_a\\lambda_{ia}x_{ia}^k,\n\\qquad D_{i,k}(p)=0\n\\quad(p\\in\\mathbb C(x_{ia})).\n\\]\nAdditivity in \\(\\lambda\\) gives the Leibniz rule. The derivation commutes with \\(W\\), preserves (5.1) and every topological homogeneous summand, and therefore descends to \\(B_0\\). It kills \\(B_0\\), since that quotient is generated by coefficients. Thus, for arbitrary \\(p\\in P^{W_{\\lambda_m}}\\),\n\\[\nS_{\\lambda_m}\\left(\np\\left[\n\\sum_{\\substack{d\\in\\Omega_m^+\\\\i(d)=i}}\n(s(d)-1)x_d^k\n-\n\\sum_{\\substack{z\\in\\Omega_m^0\\\\i(z)=i}}x_z^k\n\\right]\\right)=0.\n\\tag{5.6}\n\\]\nPower sums generate each zero-block symmetric-polynomial ring. Including arbitrary multipliers \\(p\\), (5.6) replaces every zero-block power sum by the displayed positive-slot polynomial, preserving degree.</p><p>Now fix nonempty \\(S\\subseteq\\Omega_m^+\\). Split the size array \\(s=a+b\\), with \\(b_d=s(d)\\) on \\(S\\) and zero elsewhere, and put\n\\[\n\\alpha=a-1,\\qquad\\beta=b.\n\\]\nThe topological charge of \\(\\beta\\) is nonzero and nonnegative. Therefore\n\\[\nS_\\alpha(p_a)S_\\beta(p_b)=0\\quad\\text{in }B_0\n\\]\nfor arbitrary invariant polynomial dressings, by Proposition 3.</p><p>Fix \\(a\\) and match it with a colorwise permutation \\(b'\\). The resulting coweight is \\(t-1\\), \\(t=a+b'\\), whose nonnegative sizes again sum to \\(v_i\\) at every vertex. If \\(A=\\operatorname{supp}(a)\\), \\(B'=\\operatorname{supp}(b')\\), then\n\\[\n|\\operatorname{supp}(t)|\n=|A|+|B'|-|A\\cap B'|\n=|l|-|A\\cap B'|.\n\\]\nOverlap terms lie in \\(F_{|l|-1}\\). Disjoint terms have exactly array \\(m\\), with no other orbit type at length \\(|l|\\).</p><p>For the chosen disjoint decomposition, the excess in\n\\[\nr_\\alpha r_\\beta/r_{\\lambda_m}\n\\]\nis \\(q_S\\), up to a nonzero constant sign. For an arrow from slot \\(d\\) to slot \\(e\\), the excess exponent is\n\\[\n(a_d-a_e)_++(b_d-b_e)_+\n-(a_d+b_d-a_e-b_e)_+.\n\\]\nIt is zero unless the two slots lie in \\(\\operatorname{supp}(a)\\) and \\(S\\); then it equals \\(\\min(s(d),s(e))\\). A framing weight contributes\n\\[\n(a_d-1)_++(b_d)_+-(a_d+b_d-1)_+,\n\\]\nequal to one on \\(S\\) and zero elsewhere. These are the exponents in (5.3). The sign is \\((-1)^{\\epsilon_S}\\), where\n\\[\n\\epsilon_S\n=\n\\sum_{d\\in S}w_{i(d)}\n+\n\\sum_{\\substack{d\\in S\\\\e\\in\\Omega_m^+\\setminus S}}\nn_{i(d)i(e)}\\min(s(d),s(e)).\n\\]\nIt depends only on selected and unselected counts of each type.</p><p>Let\n\\[\nH=W_{\\lambda_m},\\qquad H_S=W_\\alpha\\cap W_\\beta.\n\\]\nThe blocks of \\(H_S\\) are selected and unselected copies of each positive type, and the zero block at each color. All disjoint decompositions of the representative \\(\\lambda_m\\) form \\(H/H_S\\). Thus the leading coefficient relation is\n\\[\n\\Phi_m\\bigl(\\operatorname{Tr}_{H_S}^H(q_Sp_ap_b)\\bigr)=0,\n\\qquad\n\\operatorname{Tr}_{H_S}^H(f)=\\sum_{h\\in H/H_S}h(f).\n\\tag{5.7}\n\\]\nThe constant sign may be removed.</p><p>Choose \\(p_a,p_b\\) to depend only on their respective positive-size blocks. Their products span the \\(H_S\\)-invariant polynomial ring on positive slots, and \\(q_S\\) is \\(H_S\\)-invariant. Reynolds averaging gives\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Av}_H(q_Sf)\n=\\operatorname{Av}_H(q_S\\operatorname{Av}_{H_S}f).\n\\]\nHence all averaged cut relations with arbitrary multipliers follow from (5.7). They span \\(J_m\\cap P_m^{W_m}\\), since \\(J_m\\) is \\(W_m\\)-stable and invariants are exact in characteristic zero.</p><p>Combining zero-slot elimination and the cut relations gives graded vector-space surjections\n\\[\n\\bigoplus_{\\substack{m\\in\\mathcal M(v)\\\\|l|=L}}\n(P_m/J_m)^{W_m}[\\delta_m]\n\\twoheadrightarrow F_L/F_{L-1},\n\\tag{5.8}\n\\]\nwhere half degree \\(d\\) on the source is placed in degree \\(d+\\delta_m\\). Summing over the finite exhaustive filtration proves (5.4).</p><p>If there are no gauge slots, the empty array gives \\(A_0=B_0=\\mathbb C\\). If \\(v\\ne0,w=0\\), the full cut is \\(1\\), so every summand is zero; correspondingly \\(e^{\\mathbf1}\\) is an invertible nonzero-charge monopole and \\(B_0=0\\). The same argument applies to an unframed connected component.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 5", "orig": "Proposition 6.2 and Corollary 6.3", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Every \\(P_m/J_m\\) is finite-dimensional.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Retain the symmetric nonnegative integers \\(c_{ij}\\), framing multiplicities \\(w_i\\), and cut construction of Proposition 4.</p><p class=\"li\">Now allow any finite nonnegative array \\(m=(m_{i,s})\\), without fixing its dimension vector.</p><p class=\"li\">Define \\(P_m,J_m,W_m,h_m(q)\\) by the slot and cut formulas of Proposition 4, and put\n  \\[\n  \\|m\\|_i=\\sum_ssm_{i,s},\\qquad\n  w(m)=\\sum_{i,s}w_im_{i,s},\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  (q)_m=\\prod_{i,s}\\prod_{j=1}^{m_{i,s}}(1-q^j),\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  b(k,m')=\\sum_{i,j,s,t}c_{ij}\\min(s,t)k_{i,s}m'_{j,t},\n  \\qquad D_m=\\frac12b(m,m).\n  \\]\n  The empty array has \\(h_0=1\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Every \\(P_m/J_m\\) is finite-dimensional. For every finite array,\n\\[\n\\frac1{(q)_m}\n=\n\\sum_{k+m'=m}\nq^{w(k)+b(k,m')}\\frac{h_{m'}(q)}{(q)_k}.\n\\tag{6.1}\n\\]\nConsequently, in \\(\\mathbb C(q)[[z_i:i\\in Q_0]]\\),\n\\[\n\\sum_mz^{\\|m\\|}q^{-D_m-w(m)}h_m(q)\n=\n\\frac{\\displaystyle\\sum_m\nz^{\\|m\\|}q^{-D_m-w(m)}/(q)_m}\n{\\displaystyle\\sum_m\nz^{\\|m\\|}q^{-D_m}/(q)_m}.\n\\tag{6.7}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Finite dimensionality follows from Lemma 5.1.</p><p>Take \\(W_m\\)-invariants in the sequences of Lemma 5.2; invariants are exact over \\(\\mathbb C\\). Subsets \\(S\\) with fixed count vector \\(k\\) form one orbit, with stabilizer \\(W_k\\times W_{m'}\\). Their invariant contribution is\n\\[\nq^{c_S}\n\\operatorname{Hilb}(\\mathbb C[x_d:d\\in S]^{W_k};q)\n\\operatorname{Hilb}((P_{m'}/J_{m'})^{W_{m'}};q)\n=\nq^{w(k)+b(k,m')}\\frac{h_{m'}(q)}{(q)_k}.\n\\]\nThere is no binomial multiplicity: invariants of the sum over a subset orbit give one such contribution. The all-reachable open is the term \\(k=0\\). Since\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Hilb}(P_m^{W_m};q)=\\frac1{(q)_m},\n\\]\nsumming strata proves (6.1).</p><p>The quadratic form satisfies\n\\[\nD_{k+m'}=D_k+D_{m'}+b(k,m').\n\\]\nThis includes diagonal type entries, since slots in the separate blocks are distinct even when their types coincide. Multiply (6.1) by\n\\[\nz^{\\|m\\|}q^{-D_m-w(m)}\n\\]\nand sum over \\(m\\). Each summand factors as\n\\[\n\\frac{z^{\\|k\\|}q^{-D_k}}{(q)_k}\nz^{\\|m'\\|}q^{-D_{m'}-w(m')}h_{m'}(q).\n\\]\nFor each monomial in the \\(z_i\\), only finitely many arrays occur. Divide the resulting convolution identity by the first factor’s series, whose constant coefficient is one, to obtain (6.7).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.1", "orig": "Lemma 6.1", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The algebra \\(P_m/J_m\\) is finite-dimensional.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(m\\) be any finite array in Proposition 5, with cut ideal \\(J_m\\subseteq P_m\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The algebra \\(P_m/J_m\\) is finite-dimensional. Thus \\(h_m(q)\\) is a polynomial with nonnegative integer coefficients.</p>", "proof": "<p>For the empty array this is immediate. Suppose \\(x\\) is a common zero of the cut polynomials and one coordinate has nonzero value \\(a\\). Take\n\\[\nS=\\{d:x_d=a\\}.\n\\]\nEvery framing factor in \\(q_S(x)\\) is nonzero, and every difference across the cut is nonzero, since \\(S\\) contains all coordinates with value \\(a\\). Thus \\(q_S(x)\\ne0\\), a contradiction.</p><p>The common zero set is contained in the origin. By the Nullstellensatz, either \\(J_m=P_m\\), or a power of every variable belongs to \\(J_m\\). Both cases give a finite-dimensional quotient. Finite-group invariants preserve finite dimensionality and the nonnegative grading.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.2", "orig": "the reachability construction in the proof of Proposition 6.2", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The coefficient map identifies \\( H_{T_m}^*(U_m)=P_m/J_m, \\) and this cohomology is even.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">For the slots of \\(m\\), form a directed graph with \\(b_{de}\\) arrows in each direction between distinct slots \\(d,e\\), and \\(w_{i(d)}\\) arrows from a fixed framing vertex to \\(d\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Assign a one-dimensional space to every vertex. Let \\(E_m\\) be its representation space, and let\n  \\[\n  T_m=(\\mathbb C^\\times)^{\\Omega_m^+}\n  \\]\n  act at the slots, fixing the framing line.</p><p class=\"li\">A slot is reachable if some directed path from the framing vertex has all arrow coordinates nonzero. Let \\(U_m\\subseteq E_m\\) be the all-reachable open subset.</p><p class=\"li\">If \\(n=|\\Omega_m^+|\\), let \\(U_r\\subseteq E_m\\) consist of representations with at most \\(r\\) unreachable slots. Thus \\(U_n=E_m\\), \\(U_0=U_m\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(|S|=r\\ge1\\), let \\(Z_S\\subseteq U_r\\) be the stratum whose unreachable set is exactly \\(S\\). Let \\(k\\) record the type counts of \\(S\\), and \\(m'=m-k\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The coefficient map identifies\n\\[\nH_{T_m}^*(U_m)=P_m/J_m,\n\\tag{6.2}\n\\]\nand this cohomology is even. Each \\(Z_S\\) is smooth and closed in \\(U_r\\), is an equivariant vector bundle over \\(U_{m'}\\), and has\n\\[\nH_{T_m}^*(Z_S)\n=(P_{m'}/J_{m'})[x_d:d\\in S].\n\\tag{6.3}\n\\]\nIts normal bundle has complex rank\n\\[\nc_S=w(k)+b(k,m')\n\\tag{6.4}\n\\]\nand Euler class \\(q_S\\). There are short exact sequences\n\\[\n0\\to\n\\bigoplus_{|S|=r}H_{T_m}^{*-2c_S}(Z_S)\n\\xrightarrow{\\sum i_{S,*}}\nH_{T_m}^*(U_r)\n\\to H_{T_m}^*(U_{r-1})\\to0.\n\\tag{6.6}\n\\]\nAll constructions are compatible with \\(W_m\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Induct on the number of slots. The zero-slot case is a point.</p><p>For \\(|S|=r\\), let \\(L_S\\subseteq E_m\\) be the linear subspace setting to zero all framing arrows into \\(S\\) and all arrows from the complement into \\(S\\). These equations make all slots in \\(S\\) unreachable. Within \\(U_r\\), the complement must then be reachable, and unreachability forces exactly these incoming coordinates to vanish. Hence\n\\[\nZ_S=L_S\\cap U_r.\n\\]\nIt is smooth and closed in \\(U_r\\), and \\(U_r\\setminus U_{r-1}\\) is the disjoint union of these strata.</p><p>Restriction to the complement identifies \\(Z_S\\) with a vector bundle over \\(U_{m'}\\). Internal arrows on \\(S\\) and arrows from \\(S\\) to its complement are unrestricted. Equivariant contraction of those fibers, together with the trivial action on the base of the slot torus indexed by \\(S\\), gives (6.3) by induction.</p><p>Only incoming arrows vanish. Thus the normal rank is \\(c_S\\), and\n\\[\ne_S=\n\\prod_{d\\in S}x_d^{w_{i(d)}}\n\\prod_{\\substack{d\\in S\\\\e\\notin S}}(x_d-x_e)^{b_{de}}\n=q_S.\n\\tag{6.5}\n\\]\nMultiplication by \\(e_S\\) on (6.3) is injective. Regard it as a polynomial in the \\(S\\)-variables over the possibly nonreduced ring \\(P_{m'}/J_{m'}\\). Its term of highest total degree in those variables is the single monomial\n\\[\n\\prod_{d\\in S}x_d^{\\,w_{i(d)}+\\sum_{e\\notin S}b_{de}}\n\\]\nwith coefficient one. Multiplication by this monomial is injective over any coefficient ring, and all other terms have smaller degree in the new variables. A highest-degree argument proves injectivity, including \\(e_S=1\\).</p><p>Self-intersection gives \\(i_S^*i_{S,*}=e_S\\). Restriction to another disjoint stratum kills the Gysin image, so the direct sum of Gysin maps is injective. The equivariant Thom sequences, applied successively from \\(U_n\\) to \\(U_0\\), give (6.6). They also prove evenness and surjectivity from\n\\[\nP_m=H_{T_m}^*(E_m).\n\\]</p><p>Every class on \\(Z_S\\) is the restriction of a polynomial. Its fundamental class is the restriction of \\(q_S\\), because incoming linear equations cut it out regularly. The projection formula identifies the Gysin image with the ideal generated by that class. Removing all strata gives exactly the ideal generated by nonempty cuts, proving (6.2).</p><p>Choose arrow labels compatibly with permutations of equal types. Then \\(W_m\\) acts throughout, permuting slot tori and variables. Complex orientations make the Thom classes equivariant without permutation signs.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 6", "orig": "Corollary 6.6", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Determinant stability is regular, and the coefficient map induces a canonical isomorphism of graded \\(R_0\\)-algebras \\( B_0\\xrightarrow{\\sim}H^*(X_{\\det}(v,w)). \\) The assertion includes the empty case.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">The quiver has a loop at every vertex of positive gauge dimension, flavor is trivial, and\n  \\[\n  \\nu_{\\det}=\\prod_i\\det_{V_i}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(X_{\\det}(v,w)\\) be the corresponding zero-moment quiver variety.</p><p class=\"li\">Retain \\(C(v),D_m,l,h_m(q)\\) from Propositions 3–5, with \\(m\\in\\mathcal M(v)\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Determinant stability is regular, and the coefficient map induces a canonical isomorphism of graded \\(R_0\\)-algebras\n\\[\nB_0\\xrightarrow{\\sim}H^*(X_{\\det}(v,w)).\n\\]\nThe assertion includes the empty case.</p>", "proof": "<p>By Lemma 6.1, determinant stability is regular. Proposition 2 gives\n\\[\nB_0\\twoheadrightarrow H^*(X_{\\det}(v,w)).\n\\]\nProposition 4 bounds each graded piece of the source from above by the sum in (6.8), and Lemma 6.1 identifies that sum with the target’s ordinary Poincaré polynomial. Surjectivity gives the reverse inequalities. The kernel vanishes in every degree.</p><p>The resulting bijection is the original coefficient-compatible algebra map. It is therefore an isomorphism over \\(R_0\\), identifying the coefficient ideals and retaining nilpotents. The argument also covers the zero ring.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.1", "orig": "Proposition 6.4; determinant-stability criterion from Section 6.1", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Determinant stability is regular.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the looped, no-flavor setting of Proposition 6.</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  P_{X_{\\det}(v,w)}(q)\n  =\\sum_{j\\ge0}\\dim H^{2j}(X_{\\det}(v,w))q^j,\n  \\]\n  with polynomial zero for the empty variety.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Determinant stability is regular. Moreover,\n\\[\nP_{X_{\\det}(v,w)}(q)\n=\n\\sum_{\\|m\\|=v}\nq^{C(v)-D_m+w\\cdot(v-l)}h_m(q).\n\\tag{6.8}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Write \\(I_i:W_i\\to V_i\\) for incoming framing maps. In the convention\n\\[\nf(gx)=\\nu_{\\det}(g)^a f(x),\n\\]\nsemistability is cyclicity: the smallest doubled-quiver-invariant collection containing all \\(\\operatorname{im}I_i\\) is \\(V\\).</p><p>If that collection is proper, a one-parameter subgroup of weight zero on it and weight \\(-1\\) on a complement has a limit and negative determinant weight; all positive-weight semi-invariants vanish. Conversely, at a cyclic point choose bases from images of framing vectors under arrow paths. The product of their basis determinants is a nonzero semi-invariant of weight \\(\\nu_{\\det}\\). On its principal open, normalizing the path bases gives a slice meeting each orbit once. A semistable orbit limit is cyclic, and one such principal open meets the original orbit; the slice shows the limit is in that orbit. Stabilizers fix all path images, hence are trivial. With the opposite linearization convention, the dual argument gives cocyclicity. The argument restricts to the moment fiber and permits loops. Thus determinant stability is regular.</p><p>Give \\(N\\) a Hermitian metric invariant under\n\\[\nG_c=\\prod_iU(V_i)\n\\]\nand \\(T^*N\\) its flat hyperkähler structure. Let \\(\\eta=(1,\\ldots,1)\\) be the real central level corresponding to determinant stability. GIT–moment-map correspondence identifies \\(X_{\\det}\\) with the compact quotient at real level \\(\\eta\\) and complex level zero.</p><p>Quaternionic rotation sends this to real level zero and complex level \\(c\\eta\\), for one common nonzero normalization constant \\(c\\). At real level zero, affine Kempf–Ness identifies the quotient with\n\\[\n\\mu_{\\mathbb C}^{-1}(c\\eta)//G.\n\\]\nThese are identifications of underlying topological spaces, not holomorphic rotation in the original complex structure. The rotation acts directly on the flat representation [20, Section 3(D)]; affine Kempf–Ness is [22, Theorem 4.2(v)], restricted to the closed invariant moment fiber. Multiplying the cotangent coordinate by \\(c^{-1}\\) gives an algebraic isomorphism to\n\\[\nM_\\eta(v,w)=\\mu_{\\mathbb C}^{-1}((\\mathrm{id}_{V_i})_i)//G.\n\\]\nThus \\(M_\\eta\\) and \\(X_{\\det}\\) have the same ordinary cohomology and simultaneous emptiness.</p><p>Use Hausel’s formula [16, Equation (9)], which permits loops and multiple arrows. For multipartitions \\(\\lambda=(\\lambda^i)\\), put\n\\[\n\\langle\\lambda^i,\\lambda^j\\rangle\n=\\sum_{a,b}\\min(\\lambda_a^i,\\lambda_b^j),\n\\]\n\\[\nE_Q(\\lambda)\n=\\sum_{a:i\\to j\\in Q_1}\\langle\\lambda^i,\\lambda^j\\rangle\n-\\sum_i\\langle\\lambda^i,\\lambda^i\\rangle,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\delta(v,w)\n=\\sum_{a:i\\to j\\in Q_1}v_iv_j-\\sum_iv_i^2+w\\cdot v.\n\\]\nLet \\(m(\\lambda)_{i,s}\\) count parts of size \\(s\\). With \\(q=t^2\\), where \\(t\\) is the ordinary cohomological variable, Hausel’s formula is\n\\[\n\\sum_vq^{-\\delta(v,w)}P_{M_\\eta(v,w)}(q)z^v\n=\n\\frac{\\displaystyle\\sum_\\lambda\nz^{|\\lambda|}\nq^{-E_Q(\\lambda)-\\sum_i\\langle\\lambda^i,(1^{w_i})\\rangle}/(q)_{m(\\lambda)}}\n{\\displaystyle\\sum_\\lambda\nz^{|\\lambda|}q^{-E_Q(\\lambda)}/(q)_{m(\\lambda)}}.\n\\tag{6.9}\n\\]\nThe left side uses ordinary cohomology. Its normalization is checked by Poincaré duality: for complex dimension \\(2\\delta\\),\n\\[\nP_c(u)=u^{2\\delta}P(u^{-1}),\n\\]\nso \\(u^{-\\delta}P_c(u)\\) becomes \\(q^{-\\delta}P(q)\\) under \\(u=q^{-1}\\).</p><p>Removing one loop at each vertex cancels the vertex self-pairing, giving\n\\[\nE_Q(\\lambda)=D_m,\\qquad\n\\sum_i\\langle\\lambda^i,(1^{w_i})\\rangle\n=\\sum_iw_il_i=w(m),\n\\qquad\n\\delta(v,w)=C(v)+w\\cdot v.\n\\]\nThe ratio in (6.9) is therefore exactly (6.7). Extract its \\(z^v\\)-coefficient and multiply by \\(q^{C(v)+w\\cdot v}\\) to obtain (6.8).</p><p>Each \\(h_m\\) is finite by Proposition 5, only finitely many multipartitions occur, and\n\\[\nC(v)-D_m\n=\\frac12\\sum_{i,j,s,t}\nc_{ij}\\bigl(st-\\min(s,t)\\bigr)m_{i,s}m_{j,t}\\ge0,\n\\]\n\\[\nw\\cdot(v-l)=\\sum_{i,s}w_i(s-1)m_{i,s}\\ge0.\n\\]\nEven diagonal \\(c_{ii}\\) and paired off-diagonal terms give integrality.</p><p>For \\(v=0\\), the polynomial is one. For \\(v\\ne0,w=0\\), the full cut is \\(1\\), so the polynomial is zero, agreeing with emptiness at determinant stability. An unframed connected component gives the same unit-cut conclusion.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 7", "orig": "Theorem 7.4", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The coefficient map induces a canonical isomorphism of graded \\(R\\)-algebras \\( B_F\\xrightarrow{\\sim}H_F^*(X). \\) This is the full-topological-torus comparison and is independent of the later cocharacter comparison.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Return to arbitrary finite quivers, including loops and multiple arrows, arbitrary dimension and framing vectors, arbitrary commuting flavor torus \\(F\\), and any regular character \\(\\nu\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(X=\\mu^{-1}(0)^{\\nu\\text{-stable}}/G\\), and let\n  \\[\n  B_F=A_F/\\langle A_F^{(d)}:d\\ne0\\rangle.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The coefficient map induces a canonical isomorphism of graded \\(R\\)-algebras\n\\[\nB_F\\xrightarrow{\\sim}H_F^*(X).\n\\]\nThis is the full-topological-torus comparison and is independent of the later cocharacter comparison.</p>", "proof": "<p>At determinant stability, Lemma 7.3 identifies \\(B_0\\) with ordinary cohomology, so \\(B_0\\) is finite-dimensional. Lemma 7.1 gives the same total Betti number at every regular stability.</p><p>For any such stability, Proposition 2 gives a graded, coefficient-compatible surjection\n\\[\nB_0\\twoheadrightarrow H^*(X).\n\\]\nEquality of total dimensions makes it an isomorphism. Lemma 7.2 lifts it to every commuting flavor torus, with arbitrary commuting actions covered by Proposition 1.</p><p>If a regular quotient is empty, the same argument gives \\(B_0=B_F=0\\). Surjectivity of \\(R\\to B_F\\) makes the coefficient-compatible isomorphism unique.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.1", "orig": "Lemma 7.1", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The ordinary Betti numbers of \\(X_\\theta\\) are independent of regular \\(\\theta\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix an arbitrary finite quiver and fixed dimension and framing vectors.</p><p class=\"li\">For a regular character \\(\\theta\\), let\n  \\[\n  X_\\theta=\\mu^{-1}(0)//_\\theta G.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The ordinary Betti numbers of \\(X_\\theta\\) are independent of regular \\(\\theta\\). Empty quotients are included.</p>", "proof": "<p>By Proposition 1, every regular character has a real open ball in which rational parameters preserve the stable and semistable loci, quotient, and principal gauge bundle.</p><p>Let\n\\[\nG_c=\\prod_iU(V_i),\\qquad\n\\mathfrak c=(\\mathfrak g^*)^G\n\\]\nbe the complex central moment-parameter space. Character space is a real form of \\(\\mathfrak c\\). For rational \\(\\theta\\), clear denominators and dilate the flat representation by the inverse square root of the common denominator to return the quadratic moment maps to level \\(\\theta\\).</p><p>Kempf–Ness identifies \\(X_\\theta\\) with the compact quotient at real level \\(\\theta\\), complex level zero. Quaternionic rotation, commuting with \\(G_c\\), changes this to real level zero and complex level \\(c\\theta\\), where \\(c\\ne0\\) is fixed by normalization. Affine Kempf–Ness gives\n\\[\nX_\\theta\\simeq\\mu^{-1}(c\\theta)//G.\n\\tag{7.1}\n\\]\nThis is the direct flat-representation rotation [20, Section 3(D)], valid with loops and requiring no properness of a moment-map family. One can normalize \\(c=1\\), or identify common nonzero scalar multiples by dilation.</p><p>Consider the finite-type affine family\n\\[\n\\pi:\\mathcal Z=\n\\operatorname{Spec}\n\\mathbb C[\\{(x,\\zeta)\\in T^*N\\times\\mathfrak c:\\mu(x)=\\zeta\\}]^G\n\\longrightarrow\\mathfrak c.\n\\]\nIts fiber is \\(\\mu^{-1}(\\zeta)//G\\), without a flatness assumption. Indeed, if \\(A\\) is the coordinate ring before invariants and \\(\\mathfrak m_\\zeta\\) the parameter ideal, exactness of invariants and Reynolds averaging give\n\\[\n(A/\\mathfrak m_\\zeta A)^G\n=A^G/\\mathfrak m_\\zeta A^G.\n\\]</p><p>Constructibility of compactly supported direct image and the fiber-stalk description [31, Theorem 2.5(b); 38, Theorem 5.10] show that \\(R\\pi_!\\mathbb C_{\\mathcal Z}\\) has constructible cohomology sheaves with stalks\n\\[\nH_c^*(\\pi^{-1}(\\zeta)).\n\\]\nOn a common connected dense Zariski open \\(\\Omega\\subseteq\\mathfrak c\\), all compactly supported Betti numbers are constant. Each real open character ball is Zariski dense after multiplication by \\(c\\), and its rational points are dense in the usual topology. Thus every regular character can be perturbed without changing its quotient so that \\(c\\theta\\in\\Omega\\).</p><p>By (7.1), all regular quotients have identical compactly supported Betti numbers. A nonempty regular quotient is smooth of complex dimension\n\\[\n2(\\dim N-\\dim G),\n\\]\nbecause freeness makes the moment map a submersion along the stable locus. Poincaré duality gives equality of ordinary Betti numbers. If one regular quotient is empty, all these compactly supported groups vanish; a nonempty smooth quotient would have nonzero top compactly supported cohomology. This includes nonfaithful gauge actions and unframed components. For trivial \\(G\\), the assertion is immediate.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.2", "orig": "Lemma 7.2", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The surjection of Proposition 2 is an isomorphism of graded \\(R\\)-algebras \\( B_F\\xrightarrow{\\sim}H_F^*(X). \\)", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix an arbitrary quiver and a regular stability \\(\\nu\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Assume the coefficient map induces\n  \\[\n  B_0\\cong H^*(X).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(F\\) be any commuting flavor torus.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The surjection of Proposition 2 is an isomorphism of graded \\(R\\)-algebras\n\\[\nB_F\\xrightarrow{\\sim}H_F^*(X).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Set \\(S=\\mathbb C[\\mathfrak f]\\) and let \\(S_+\\) be its positive-degree ideal. Proposition 1 gives \\(H^{\\mathrm{odd}}(X)=0\\). The Borel spectral sequence has second page\n\\[\nH^*(BF)\\otimes H^*(X)\n\\]\nconcentrated in even total degrees. It degenerates, and the edge map to ordinary cohomology is surjective. Lift a finite homogeneous basis and apply Leray–Hirsch. Then \\(H_F^*(X)\\) is free over \\(S\\), with\n\\[\nH_F^*(X)/S_+H_F^*(X)=H^*(X).\n\\]\nThis includes zero modules when \\(X=\\varnothing\\).</p><p>Let \\(J\\) be the kernel of \\(B_F\\twoheadrightarrow H_F^*(X)\\). Freeness kills\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Tor}_1^S(H_F^*(X),S/S_+),\n\\]\nso tensoring the defining sequence gives\n\\[\n0\\to J/S_+J\n\\to B_F/S_+B_F\n\\to H^*(X)\\to0.\n\\]\nFlavor base change in Proposition 1 identifies the middle map with the assumed isomorphism \\(B_0\\to H^*(X)\\). Hence \\(J=S_+J\\).</p><p>The module \\(J\\) is graded and bounded below, since \\(B_F\\) is a quotient of nonnegatively graded \\(R\\). A nonzero homogeneous element of least degree cannot be expressed through \\(S_+J\\), whose coefficients have positive degree. Thus \\(J=0\\). No nonnegative grading on \\(A_F\\) is used.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.3", "orig": "Proposition 7.3", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The coefficient map induces an isomorphism of graded \\(R_0\\)-algebras \\( B_0\\xrightarrow{\\sim}H^*(X). \\)", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(Q\\) be any finite quiver with any dimension and framing vectors.</p><p class=\"li\">Flavor is trivial and stability is \\(\\nu_{\\det}=\\prod_i\\det_{V_i}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(X\\) be the corresponding zero-moment quiver variety.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The coefficient map induces an isomorphism of graded \\(R_0\\)-algebras\n\\[\nB_0\\xrightarrow{\\sim}H^*(X).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Discard zero-rank factors and add one loop at every remaining vertex:\n\\[\nN^+=N\\oplus\\bigoplus_i\\operatorname{End}(V_i).\n\\]\nLet \\(H=\\mathbb C^\\times\\) act trivially on \\(N\\) and with weight one on each added loop. Write\n\\[\nH_H^*(\\mathrm{pt})=\\mathbb C[z],\\qquad\\deg z=2,\n\\]\nand use superscript \\(+\\) for the enlarged theory. Proposition 6 and Lemma 7.2 give\n\\[\nB_H^+\\xrightarrow{\\sim}H_H^*(X^+)\n\\quad\\text{over }R_0[z].\n\\tag{7.2}\n\\]</p><p>Let \\(\\mathfrak m_0\\subset R_0\\) be the maximal ideal of the graded origin and set\n\\[\n\\Lambda=R_0[z]_{(\\mathfrak m_0,z-1)}.\n\\tag{7.3}\n\\]\nThis localization forgets cohomological grading but retains charge.</p><p>For the Coulomb comparison, use the common Laurent realization of Proposition 1. For a positive or negative minuscule \\(\\lambda\\), the added numerator is\n\\[\nE_\\lambda(x,z)=\n\\prod_i\\prod_{a,b=1}^{v_i}\n(z+x_{ib}-x_{ia})^{(\\lambda_{ia}-\\lambda_{ib})_+}.\n\\]\nIt is \\(W_\\lambda\\)-invariant and equals one at \\((x,z)=(0,1)\\). Its inverse belongs to\n\\[\nP[z]^{W_\\lambda}\\otimes_{R_0[z]}\\Lambda:\n\\]\nindeed,\n\\[\n\\Delta_\\lambda=\\prod_{w\\in W}wE_\\lambda\\in R_0[z],\n\\qquad \\Delta_\\lambda(0,1)=1,\n\\qquad\nE_\\lambda^{-1}=(\\Delta_\\lambda/E_\\lambda)/\\Delta_\\lambda.\n\\]\nAn enlarged generator with dressing \\(p\\) is the original \\(M(\\lambda,pE_\\lambda)\\); dressing by \\(p/E_\\lambda\\) recovers the original generator after localization. Generation identifies the localized Coulomb algebras, their coefficient maps, and full topological gradings. Since inverted elements have charge zero, quotienting by nonzero charges commutes with localization:\n\\[\nB_H^+\\otimes_{R_0[z]}\\Lambda\n\\cong\n(B_0\\otimes\\mathbb C[z])\\otimes_{R_0[z]}\\Lambda.\n\\tag{7.4}\n\\]</p><p>For the cohomological comparison, let \\(D^+\\subset T^*N^+\\) be the stable zero-moment locus. Localize the actual \\(H\\)-fixed locus of the prequotient, retaining \\(G\\times H\\)-equivariance; quotient-fixed points could otherwise involve compensating gauge transformations.</p><p>First restrict to \\(T\\times H\\). Brion’s subgroup localization theorem [6, Lemma 4] makes\n\\[\nH_{T\\times H}^*(D^+)\\to H_{T\\times H}^*((D^+)^H)\n\\]\nan isomorphism after inverting finitely many characters nontrivial on \\(H\\). Every such character is nonzero at \\((0,1)\\). Taking Weyl invariants recovers \\(G\\times H\\)-equivariance. Products of Weyl translates give invariant denominators still nonzero at this Weyl-fixed point, so the comparison is valid over \\(\\Lambda\\). This is the central-parameter prequotient localization of [13, Proposition 2.3].</p><p>All added loops and their cotangent partners have nonzero \\(H\\)-weights, hence vanish on \\((D^+)^H\\). The moment map restricts to the original one, and stability restricts to original stability: Hilbert–Mumford limits and weights are unchanged under the closed equivariant inclusion; equivalently semi-invariants lift and orbit closures stay in the subvariety. Therefore\n\\[\n(D^+)^H=D,\n\\]\nwith trivial \\(H\\)-action. Freeness gives the coefficient-compatible identification\n\\[\nH_H^*(X^+)\\otimes_{R_0[z]}\\Lambda\n\\cong\n(H^*(X)\\otimes\\mathbb C[z])\\otimes_{R_0[z]}\\Lambda.\n\\tag{7.5}\n\\]\nNo compactness is required.</p><p>For descent, let \\(I_B,I_X\\subseteq R_0\\) be the two coefficient kernels. Equations (7.2), (7.4), and (7.5) identify quotients over the same coefficient ring, so\n\\[\nI_B\\Lambda=I_X\\Lambda.\n\\]\nSet \\(z=1\\), using\n\\[\n\\Lambda/(z-1)\\Lambda=(R_0)_{\\mathfrak m_0},\n\\]\nto obtain equality localized at the graded origin.</p><p>Both ideals are homogeneous. If homogeneous \\(h\\in I_B\\), there is \\(s\\in R_0\\) with \\(s(0)\\ne0\\) and \\(sh\\in I_X\\). Its degree-\\(\\deg h\\) component is \\(s(0)h\\), giving \\(h\\in I_X\\). Interchanging the ideals gives global equality. The resulting isomorphism is graded and retains nilpotents. Empty loci require no change; no gauge ranks is immediate.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 8", "orig": "Lemma 8.2", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There is a finite expression \\( M^\\epsilon_{b,p} =\\sum_r c_r\\,a_{r1}\\cdots a_{rk_r}, \\qquad c_r\\in R_{\\Gamma,d,F}, \\) such that every factor is topologically homogeneous and …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\Gamma\\) be an unframed finite quiver, allowing loops, with gauge dimension vector \\(d\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(F\\) act by a character on each arrow. Write \\(A_{\\Gamma,d,F}\\) and \\(R_{\\Gamma,d,F}\\) for its commutative Coulomb and coefficient algebras.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(A_{\\Gamma,\\gamma}(q)\\) be the Kac polynomial counting absolutely indecomposable representations, and define its nonzero support\n  \\[\n  \\Delta_\\Gamma=\n  \\{0\\ne\\gamma\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}^{\\Gamma_0}:\n      A_{\\Gamma,\\gamma}(q)\\ne0\\}.\n  \\]\n  No loopless root-system interpretation is assumed.</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(\\epsilon\\in\\{1,-1\\}\\) and \\(0&lt;b\\le d\\), let \\(M^\\epsilon_{b,p}\\) be the dressed minuscule class whose coweight has \\(b_i\\) entries \\(\\epsilon\\) at vertex \\(i\\) and all other entries zero. The dressing is invariant under that coweight’s Weyl stabilizer.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a finite expression\n\\[\nM^\\epsilon_{b,p}\n=\\sum_r c_r\\,a_{r1}\\cdots a_{rk_r},\n\\qquad c_r\\in R_{\\Gamma,d,F},\n\\tag{8.3}\n\\]\nsuch that every factor is topologically homogeneous and\n\\[\n\\deg_{\\mathrm{top}}a_{rh}=\\epsilon\\gamma_{rh},\n\\qquad\n\\gamma_{rh}\\in\\Delta_\\Gamma,\n\\qquad\n\\sum_{h=1}^{k_r}\\gamma_{rh}=b.\n\\]\nThe factors need not be minuscule.</p>", "proof": "<p>Use the independent polynomial ring \\(S\\) of Lemma 8.1, the equivariant coefficient ring of\n\\[\nT_{\\mathrm{univ}}=(\\mathbb C^\\times)^{1+|\\Gamma_1|}.\n\\]\nIt is in the geometric parameter setting of [23, Equation (30) and Remark 2.6]. On the tripled quiver, use weights\n\\[\n-u_a,\\qquad u_a-\\hbar,\\qquad\\hbar\n\\]\non an original arrow, its reverse, and an added loop. These satisfy [23, Section 2.2], including absence of nonzero nonnegative integral relations among \\(u_a,\\hbar-u_a\\), and \\(u_a\\notin\\mathbb Z\\hbar\\) for an original loop.</p><p>Use the polynomial-form Hall results:</p><p class=\"li\">loop-nilpotent Hall algebra identifies with its shuffle half [23, Proposition 3.7];</p><p class=\"li\">BPS descendants generate over \\(S\\) [23, Theorem 3.10, Equation (109), and Corollary 3.15];</p><p class=\"li\">BPS comparison and the Kac formula give nonzero dimension-vector support \\(\\Delta_\\Gamma\\) [23, Proposition 3.14 and Section 3.10].</p><p>The Kac identity retains the full cohomological grading: the coefficient in cohomological degree \\(j\\) is \\((-1)^j\\dim H^j\\). A zero Kac polynomial forces all graded pieces of the corresponding BPS space to vanish, and the cited comparison transfers this to loop-nilpotent BPS spaces. The twist preserves homogeneous word spans. Polynomial form concerns equivariant parameters over \\(\\mathbb Q\\), not integral cohomology.</p><p>Apply generation to \\(e_{b,g}\\) or \\(f_{b,g}\\) and retain dimension degree \\(b\\). All nonconstant Hall degrees are nonnegative, giving a finite \\(S\\)-linear sum of words in BPS descendants with degrees in \\(\\Delta_\\Gamma\\) summing to \\(b\\). Lemma 8.1 maps this to a finite identity in \\(\\mathcal C_S\\), without parameter inverses. Every factor has degree at most \\(b\\le d\\).</p><p>Extend scalars to \\(\\mathbb C\\) and specialize by Lemma 8.1. Each finite identity becomes an identity in \\(A_{\\Gamma,d,F}\\); nonzero constant signs are absorbed in coefficients. Specialization is performed on the abstract polynomial-form algebra and its map, not its ambient polynomial representative. An ambient representative divisible by \\(\\hbar\\) need not belong to \\(\\hbar\\) times the polynomial shuffle half and is not discarded before mapping.</p><p>For an arbitrary dressing \\(p\\), the ring symmetric separately in selected and complementary variables is generated by total symmetric polynomials and selected-variable symmetric polynomials: a complementary power sum is total minus selected. Thus \\(p\\) is a finite sum of coefficient polynomials times selected-variable dressings \\(g\\). The identities already obtained give (8.3).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 8.1", "orig": "Lemma 8.1", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The two shuffle halves admit \\(S\\)-linear algebra maps into \\(\\mathcal C_S\\), without inverting rotation or arrow parameters.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the unframed quiver \\(\\Gamma\\) and dimension vector \\(d\\) of Proposition 8.</p><p class=\"li\">Introduce independent parameters\n  \\[\n  S=\\mathbb Q[\\hbar,u_a:a\\in\\Gamma_1],\n  \\qquad K=\\operatorname{Frac}S.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mathcal S_S^+\\) be the polynomial shuffle half of [23, Definition 2.3], with product \\(*\\), graded by dimension vectors. It contains\n  \\[\n  e_{n,g}\n  =\n  g(z_{ia})\\prod_i\\prod_{a,b=1}^{n_i}(z_{ib}-z_{ia}+\\hbar),\n  \\qquad\n  g\\in S[z_{ia}]^{\\prod_iS_{n_i}}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For homogeneous dimensions \\(n,m\\), use\n  \\[\n  E\\star E'\n  =(-1)^{\\sum_{a:i\\to j}n_jm_i}\\,E*E'.\n  \\tag{8.1}\n  \\]\n  Let \\(\\mathcal S_S^-\\) be its ordinary opposite algebra, with \\(f_{n,g}\\) the corresponding distinguished polynomial.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(D_{ia}\\) be commuting shifts satisfying\n  \\[\n  D_{ia}w_{jb}\n  =(w_{jb}+\\hbar\\delta_{ij}\\delta_{ab})D_{ia}.\n  \\]\n  Allow coefficients in\n  \\[\n  S[w_{ia},(w_{ia}-w_{ib}+r\\hbar)^{-1}:a\\ne b,\\ r\\in\\mathbb Z].\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For subsets \\(A_i\\subseteq\\{1,\\ldots,d_i\\}\\), write \\(w_A=(w_{ia})_{a\\in A_i}\\), and define\n  \\[\n  E_{n,g}\n  =\n  \\sum_{|A_i|=n_i}\n  g(w_A)\n  \\frac{\\displaystyle\n  \\prod_{a:i\\to j}\n  \\prod_{\\alpha\\in A_i,\\ \\beta\\notin A_j}\n  (w_{i\\alpha}-w_{j\\beta}+u_a)}\n  {\\displaystyle\n  \\prod_i\\prod_{\\alpha\\in A_i,\\ \\beta\\notin A_i}\n  (w_{i\\alpha}-w_{i\\beta})}\n  \\prod_{i,\\alpha\\in A_i}D_{i\\alpha},\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  F_{n,g}\n  =\n  \\sum_{|A_i|=n_i}\n  g(w_A-\\hbar)\n  \\frac{\\displaystyle\n  \\prod_{a:j\\to i}\n  \\prod_{\\alpha\\in A_i,\\ \\beta\\notin A_j}\n  (w_{i\\alpha}-w_{j\\beta}-u_a)}\n  {\\displaystyle\n  \\prod_i\\prod_{\\alpha\\in A_i,\\ \\beta\\notin A_i}\n  (w_{i\\alpha}-w_{i\\beta})}\n  \\prod_{i,\\alpha\\in A_i}D_{i\\alpha}^{-1}.\n  \\]\n  Let \\(\\mathcal C_S\\) be the \\(S\\)-algebra generated by these operators and polynomials symmetric in the \\(w\\)-variables at each vertex.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The two shuffle halves admit \\(S\\)-linear algebra maps into \\(\\mathcal C_S\\), without inverting rotation or arrow parameters. They send \\(e_{n,g}\\) and \\(f_{n,g}\\), for \\(n\\le d\\), to nonzero constant multiples of \\(E_{n,g}\\) and \\(F_{n,g}\\), respectively, and send them to zero when \\(n\\not\\le d\\).</p><p>After specializing\n\\[\n\\hbar=0,\\qquad w_{ia}=x_{ia},\\qquad u_a=-f_a,\n\\]\nwhere \\(f_a\\) is the arrow’s prescribed flavor weight, their images lie in \\(A_{\\Gamma,d,F}\\). Dimension degree becomes positive, respectively negative, topological charge. Finite polynomial-coefficient identities survive specialization.</p>", "proof": "<p>The twist (8.1) is associative by bilinearity of its exponent. It changes homogeneous words only by dimension-dependent signs, so their \\(S\\)-spans are unchanged. The permitted coefficient ring is preserved by all shifts \\(D_{ia}^{\\pm1}\\).</p><p>Construct maps first over \\(K\\). Use Tsymbaliuk’s trigonometric shuffle maps and their opposite [41, Theorem 5.7, Equations (93) and (96)], with no framing, and substitute\n\\[\nq=e^{\\varepsilon\\hbar},\n\\qquad t_a=e^{\\varepsilon u_a},\n\\qquad W_{ia}=e^{\\varepsilon w_{ia}},\n\\]\nwhere \\(\\varepsilon\\) is auxiliary, not \\(\\hbar\\).</p><p>The multiplicative shift satisfies\n\\[\nD^{\\mathrm{trig}}_{ia}W_{ia}\n=q^{-1}W_{ia}D^{\\mathrm{trig}}_{ia}.\n\\]\nIts inverse becomes the positive additive shift \\(D_{ia}\\), and it becomes \\(D_{ia}^{-1}\\) in the negative map. The leading trigonometric kernel is\n\\[\n\\left(\\frac{x-\\hbar}{x}\\right)^{\\delta_{ij}}\n\\prod_{a:i\\to j}[-(x+u_a)]\n\\prod_{a:j\\to i}(x+\\hbar-u_a).\n\\]\nThis is exactly the kernel for \\(\\star\\): the incoming-arrow signs are removed from [23, Equation (31)]. For one arrow \\(i\\to j\\), the untwisted product gives\n\\[\ne_{(1,0),1}*e_{(0,1),1}\n=-e_{(1,1),z_i-z_j+u_a},\n\\]\nwhereas the fundamental-operator product has a plus sign.</p><p>To justify leading coefficients, including loops and repeated slots, put\n\\[\n\\kappa(n)=\\sum_{a:i\\to j}n_in_j,\\qquad\n\\ell_i=\\#\\{a:i\\to i\\},\n\\]\n\\[\nL_i^+=\\sum_{a:i\\to j}d_j-d_i+1,\n\\qquad\nL_i^-=\\sum_{a:j\\to i}d_j-d_i+1.\n\\]\nLift a polynomial \\(E\\) of dimension \\(n\\) to\n\\[\n\\varepsilon^{\\kappa(n)}E((X_{ia}-1)/\\varepsilon).\n\\]\nCross-kernel order is \\(\\kappa(n+m)-\\kappa(n)-\\kappa(m)\\), so the leading source product is \\(\\star\\).</p><p>In an operator summand, let \\(m_{ia}\\) be the string length assigned to gauge slot \\((i,a)\\), so \\(\\sum_am_{ia}=n_i\\), and put\n\\[\nP_2=\\sum_{i,a}\\ell_i m_{ia}^2.\n\\]\nThe three prefactors in Tsymbaliuk’s formulas have \\(\\varepsilon\\)-orders\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c}\n\\text{single-entry factors }Y\\text{ or }Y'&amp;\nL^\\pm\\cdot n-\\sum_i\\ell_in_i\\\\\n\\text{inverse kernels within one string}&amp;\n-P_2+\\sum_i\\ell_in_i\\\\\n\\text{factors between distinct slots}&amp;\n-\\kappa(n)+P_2.\n\\end{array}\n\\]\nTogether with the input lift, the total order is \\(L^\\pm\\cdot n\\), independent of the composition. Multiply by \\(\\varepsilon^{-L^\\pm\\cdot n}\\) and take the constant coefficient. Remaining denominators have nonzero leading terms over \\(K(w)\\), and only finitely many compositions occur. Thus zero normalized leading input has zero normalized leading output, making the construction independent of the lift. Additivity of \\(L^\\pm\\cdot n\\) gives multiplicativity. Rescaling in degree \\(n\\) by \\(\\hbar^{-|n|}\\) preserves multiplicativity.</p><p>For real \\(\\hbar&lt;u_a&lt;0\\) and sufficiently small positive \\(\\varepsilon\\), the substitutions satisfy Tsymbaliuk’s parameter inequalities. Monomial factors involving logarithms expand near \\(W=1\\), with leading coefficient one and rational Taylor coefficients. Every multiplication coefficient is a rational identity on a nonempty real open set, hence an identity over \\(K(w)\\). This justifies the formal leading maps \\(\\Psi_+,\\Psi_-\\).</p><p>For distinguished elements,\n\\[\n\\Psi_+(e_{n,g})\n=(-1)^{L^+\\cdot n+\\kappa(n)}E_{n,g},\n\\qquad\n\\Psi_-(f_{n,g})=F_{n,g}.\n\\tag{8.2}\n\\]\nThe same-color factor vanishes on every additive string of length at least two, leaving only subsets of distinct slots. Its diagonal factors give \\(\\hbar^{|n|}\\), canceling the normalization; off-diagonal factors cancel same-color denominators. Arrow factors between selected slots cancel as well. Positive single-entry factors contribute\n\\[\n(-1)^{\\sum_i n_i(L_i^+-\\ell_i)},\n\\]\nand internal arrows contribute\n\\[\n(-1)^{\\kappa(n)-\\sum_i\\ell_in_i},\n\\]\ngiving the displayed sign. In the opposite formula the selected variables are \\(w_A-\\hbar\\), and incoming-arrow factors cancel without these signs. No subset exists when \\(n\\not\\le d\\).</p><p>The distinguished elements generate \\(\\mathcal S_S^+\\) over \\(S\\) [23, Theorem 2.4]. The twist preserves word spans, and reversing words gives the opposite statement. Thus (8.2) shows that the polynomial halves land in \\(\\mathcal C_S\\), although constructed over \\(K\\). No parameter inverse is needed in these identities.</p><p>Finally specialize \\(\\hbar=0,w=x,u_a=-f_a\\). Every allowed denominator becomes \\(x_{ia}-x_{ib}\\), \\(a\\ne b\\), so specialization defines a homomorphism to the rational Laurent algebra, sending \\(D_{ia}\\) to the standard slot monomial. Direct comparison with (2.2) gives\n\\[\nM_{n,g}^+\n=(-1)^{\\sum_{a:i\\to j}n_i(d_j-n_j)}\nE_{n,g}|_{\\mathrm{specialization}},\n\\]\n\\[\nM_{n,g}^-\n=(-1)^{\\sum_i n_i(d_i-n_i)}\nF_{n,g}|_{\\mathrm{specialization}}.\n\\]\nThe first sign is from outgoing matter factors and the second from reversing tangent weights. Every generator of \\(\\mathcal C_S\\), and hence every image of the polynomial halves, specializes into the Coulomb algebra. No injectivity or flatness after specialization is asserted.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 9", "orig": "Proposition 8.4", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The natural quotient map \\( (A_F)_{\\mathrm{fix},\\nu}\\longrightarrow B_F \\) is an isomorphism.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the arbitrary quiver, framing, commuting flavor, and regular character \\(\\nu\\) of Theorem 1.</p><p class=\"li\">Write\n  \\[\n  B_F=A_F/\\langle A_F^{(d)}:d\\ne0\\rangle,\n  \\qquad\n  (A_F)_{\\mathrm{fix},\\nu}\n  =A_F/\\langle(A_F)_m:m\\ne0\\rangle.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The natural quotient map\n\\[\n(A_F)_{\\mathrm{fix},\\nu}\\longrightarrow B_F\n\\]\nis an isomorphism. Equivalently, the defining ideals generated by nonzero cocharacter weights and by nonzero full topological charges coincide in \\(A_F\\). The isomorphism preserves coefficients and both gradings.</p><p>This comparison does not use Proposition 7 or assume coefficient surjectivity onto the cocharacter-fixed quotient.</p>", "proof": "<p>Proposition 1 allows reduction to flavor characters on individual arrows and framing rows, without changing regularity or fixed quotients. Write\n\\[\n\\nu((g_i))=\\prod_i\\det(g_i)^{\\nu_i}.\n\\]</p><p>Adjoin a vertex \\(\\infty\\) of dimension one and \\(w_i\\) arrows \\(i\\to\\infty\\). Denote the resulting unframed quiver by \\(\\widehat Q\\), with dimension vector\n\\[\nd=(v,1).\n\\]\nAfter choosing framing bases, its representation is the original \\(N\\). The group isomorphism\n\\[\nG\\times\\mathbb C^\\times\\longrightarrow G_d,\n\\qquad\n((g_i),c)\\longmapsto((cg_i),c)\n\\]\nmakes the second factor act trivially. The BFN space and convolution split:\n\\[\nA_{\\widehat Q,d,F}\n\\cong A_F\\otimes\\mathbb C[\\xi,z,z^{-1}].\n\\tag{8.4}\n\\]\nHere \\(\\xi\\) is the extra scalar coefficient, and \\(z\\) is its invertible fundamental monopole of cohomological degree zero. This \\(z\\) is unrelated to the degree-two mass in Lemma 7.3. In enlarged vertex coordinates, it has charge \\(d\\), translating every coweight entry by one.</p><p>Extend the character by\n\\[\n\\widehat\\nu_i=\\nu_i\\quad(i\\in Q_0),\n\\qquad\n\\widehat\\nu_\\infty=-\\sum_i\\nu_iv_i.\n\\]\nThen \\(\\widehat\\nu\\cdot d=0\\), and under the group isomorphism it is \\(\\nu\\) on \\(G\\), trivial on the extra scalar. Both \\(\\xi,z^{\\pm1}\\) have weight zero. Hence\n\\[\n(A_{\\widehat Q,d,F})_{\\mathrm{fix},\\widehat\\nu}\n\\cong\n(A_F)_{\\mathrm{fix},\\nu}\\otimes\\mathbb C[\\xi,z,z^{-1}],\n\\]\na faithfully flat extension.</p><p>Let \\(M^+_{b',p}\\) be a nontrivial positive minuscule generator of \\(A_F\\), and put \\(b=(b',0)\\). If \\(\\widehat x_{ia}\\) are enlarged roots, original roots identify as\n\\[\nx_{ia}=\\widehat x_{ia}-\\xi.\n\\]\nLet \\(p^\\uparrow\\) be obtained by this substitution, leaving flavor parameters unchanged. The original generator becomes \\(M^+_{b,p^\\uparrow}\\). Central translation gives the complementary identity\n\\[\nM^+_{b,p^\\uparrow}\n=z\\,M^-_{d-b,p^\\uparrow}.\n\\tag{8.5}\n\\]\nThe coweights have the same Weyl stabilizer, so the dressing is admissible on both sides. It need not depend only on the complementary selected variables; Proposition 8 permits arbitrary stabilizer-invariant dressings.</p><p>Equation (8.5) follows from (2.2): matter weights pair trivially with the central coweight, and central translation identifies the minuscule orbits and tangent bundles. Equivalently, convolve with the invertible central class. Compatible fundamental-class conventions give the equality; rescaling changes only a nonzero constant.</p><p>Suppose this positive generator survives in the cocharacter-fixed quotient. Apply Proposition 8 to \\(M^+_{b,p^\\uparrow}\\). A word containing a nonzero \\(\\widehat\\nu\\)-weight factor vanishes in that quotient. A surviving word therefore yields\n\\[\nb=\\sum_a\\alpha_a,\\qquad\n\\alpha_a\\in\\Delta_{\\widehat Q},\\qquad\n\\widehat\\nu\\cdot\\alpha_a=0.\n\\]\nSince \\(z\\) is invertible of weight zero, the complementary negative class also survives. The negative half of Proposition 8 gives\n\\[\nd-b=\\sum_b\\beta_b,\\qquad\n\\beta_b\\in\\Delta_{\\widehat Q},\\qquad\n\\widehat\\nu\\cdot\\beta_b=0.\n\\]\nBoth sums are nonempty: \\(b\\ne0\\), and its \\(\\infty\\)-entry is zero whereas that of \\(d\\) is one.</p><p>Together they express \\(d\\) as a sum of at least two nonzero vectors\n\\[\n\\gamma_1,\\ldots,\\gamma_k\\in\\Delta_{\\widehat Q},\n\\qquad\n\\widehat\\nu\\cdot\\gamma_a=0.\n\\]\nFor each choose the point supplied by Lemma 9.1 at real parameter \\(\\widehat\\nu\\). Take orthogonal vertex decompositions and their direct sum. Moment maps are block diagonal, so the resulting point has\n\\[\n\\mu_{\\mathbb C}=0,\\qquad\n\\mu_{\\mathbb R}=\\tau_{\\widehat\\nu},\n\\]\nand stabilizer containing \\(k\\) independent block scalar circles.</p><p>The enlarged reduction is the original framed reduction: the extra diagonal scalar is ineffective, its moment equation is redundant, and \\(\\widehat\\nu\\) is trivial on it. Removing that scalar leaves a stabilizer torus of dimension \\(k-1&gt;0\\). Character-level affine Kempf–Ness makes the resulting original point \\(\\nu\\)-semistable [27, Theorem 6.1 and Corollary 6.2], contradicting freeness. If the stable locus is empty, it instead produces a forbidden semistable point.</p><p>Thus every nontrivial positive minuscule generator vanishes in the cocharacter quotient. For a negative generator, use\n\\[\nM^-_{b,p^\\uparrow}\n=z^{-1}M^+_{d-b,p^\\uparrow}\n\\]\nand interchange the two halves; it also vanishes.</p><p>By Proposition 1, these generators generate the ideal of nonzero full topological charges. Hence that ideal is contained in the nonzero-cocharacter-weight ideal. Conversely, a nonzero cocharacter-weight space is a sum of nonzero topological-charge spaces. The ideals coincide. If all gauge ranks vanish, there are no generators and the conclusion is immediate.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 9.1", "orig": "Lemma 8.3", "depth": 1, "snippet": "One has \\( \\mu_{\\mathbb C}^{-1}(0) \\cap\\mu_{\\mathbb R}^{-1}(\\tau_\\theta)\\ne\\varnothing. \\) The scalar circle acting simultaneously on all nonzero vertex spaces fixes every point of this representation.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\Gamma\\) be an unframed finite quiver, allowing loops, and let \\(\\gamma\\in\\Delta_\\Gamma\\), with \\(\\Delta_\\Gamma\\) defined by Kac-polynomial support.</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  G_\\gamma=\\prod_{i\\in\\Gamma_0}\\mathrm{GL}_{\\gamma_i},\n  \\qquad\n  N_\\gamma=\\bigoplus_{a:i\\to j}\n  \\operatorname{Hom}(\\mathbb C^{\\gamma_i},\\mathbb C^{\\gamma_j}).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\theta=(\\theta_i)\\) be real with \\(\\theta\\cdot\\gamma=0\\), and define\n  \\[\n  \\tau_\\theta(\\xi)=\\sum_i\\theta_i\\operatorname{tr}(\\xi_i).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Choose standard Hermitian forms and the flat hyperkähler moment maps \\((\\mu_{\\mathbb R},\\mu_{\\mathbb C})\\) on \\(T^*N_\\gamma\\), normalized so integral \\(\\theta\\) corresponds to real level \\(\\tau_\\theta\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>One has\n\\[\n\\mu_{\\mathbb C}^{-1}(0)\n\\cap\\mu_{\\mathbb R}^{-1}(\\tau_\\theta)\\ne\\varnothing.\n\\]\nThe scalar circle acting simultaneously on all nonzero vertex spaces fixes every point of this representation.</p>", "proof": "<p>First \\(\\Gamma\\) has an indecomposable complex representation \\(x\\) of dimension \\(\\gamma\\). To pass from Kac support to characteristic zero, a representation is decomposable over an algebraic closure exactly when its endomorphism algebra contains an idempotent other than \\(0,1\\). The idempotent equations with these open exclusions give a constructible incidence condition over \\(\\mathbb Z\\). Chevalley’s theorem gives a constructible geometrically indecomposable locus. A nonzero Kac polynomial supplies points in infinitely many residue characteristics. A constructible subset of \\(\\operatorname{Spec}\\mathbb Z\\) omitting the generic point is finite; hence the characteristic-zero locus is nonempty, and base change gives \\(x\\) over \\(\\mathbb C\\).</p><p>Use the trace-annihilator lifting criterion [7, Lemma 3.1 and Theorem 3.3]. Every \\(e\\in\\operatorname{End}_\\Gamma(x)\\) has form \\(c\\,\\mathrm{id}+n\\), with \\(n\\) nilpotent: generalized eigenspaces are subrepresentations, and indecomposability allows one eigenvalue. Therefore\n\\[\n\\tau_\\theta(e)=c\\,\\theta\\cdot\\gamma=0.\n\\]</p><p>With \\(x\\) fixed, the cotangent moment map\n\\[\nN_\\gamma^*\\to\\operatorname{Lie}(G_\\gamma)^*,\n\\qquad\ny\\mapsto(\\xi\\mapsto y(\\xi x))\n\\]\nis the transpose of the infinitesimal orbit map. Its image is the annihilator of \\(\\operatorname{End}_\\Gamma(x)\\). Hence the complex moment fiber at \\(\\tau_\\theta\\), and at any common scalar multiple, is nonempty.</p><p>This central moment fiber is closed and invariant. An orbit closure in it contains an orbit closed in the ambient affine representation. Zero-character affine Kempf–Ness makes that orbit meet \\(\\mu_{\\mathbb R}^{-1}(0)\\) [27, Theorem 6.1 and Corollary 6.2]. Thus there is a point with hyperkähler moment value \\((0,\\tau_\\theta,0)\\), after the common normalization adjustment.</p><p>Quaternionic rotation commutes with the compact gauge group and rotates the three moment maps. One rotation sends this value to \\((\\tau_\\theta,0,0)\\) [20, Section 3(D)], proving nonemptiness. Simultaneous scalar acts trivially on all arrow spaces and their duals.</p>"}], "edges": [[1, 0, "cite"], [1, 7, "cite"], [1, 8, "cite"], [1, 12, "cite"], [1, 13, "cite"], [1, 19, "cite"], [1, 20, "cite"], [1, 21, "cite"], [1, 22, "cite"], [1, 25, "cite"], [2, 1, "cite"], [2, 3, "cite"], [3, 1, "cite"], [4, 1, "cite"], [5, 1, "cite"], [5, 6, "cite"], [6, 1, "cite"], [7, 17, "cite"], [7, 19, "cite"], [7, 21, "cite"], [8, 7, "cite"], [8, 10, "cite"], [8, 11, "cite"], [9, 10, "cite"], [9, 11, "cite"], [10, 7, "cite"], [10, 11, "cite"], [11, 7, "cite"], [12, 13, "cite"], [12, 17, "cite"], [13, 14, "cite"], [13, 17, "cite"], [14, 17, "cite"], [14, 18, "cite"], [15, 14, "cite"], [16, 14, "cite"], [17, 22, "cite"], [18, 17, "cite"], [19, 0, "cite"], [19, 25, "cite"], [20, 19, "cite"], [21, 19, "cite"], [21, 22, "cite"], [22, 19, "cite"], [22, 25, "cite"], [23, 25, "cite"], [24, 23, "cite"], [25, 0, "cite"], [26, 25, "cite"]]});
